window.LAB_DATA = {"chapters":[{"id":"ch7","title":"Chapter 7 · Logical Agents","sections":[{"id":"ch7-s1","title":"7.1 Knowledge-based agents","dir":"ch7/s1","slides":[{"n":1,"title":"Logical Agents","marks":[],"caps":[[0.07,"Welcome, everyone."],[0.83,"Today we start Chapter 7:"],[2.74,"Logical Agents."],[3.51,"If this is your first time seeing logic in a computer science course,"],[8.47,"you are in exactly the right place."],[11.14,"We will build every idea slowly, one small step at a time,"],[15.72,"and every slide adds just one new thing."],[18.77,"So far, our agents solved problems by searching."],[21.82,"Today we build agents that know things, and that can work out new facts"],[27.17,"they were never told."]]},{"n":2,"title":"Think like a detective","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,5.94,5.04],"t":6.08},{"k":"box","g":[7.43,1.43,5.34,5.04],"t":16.09}],"caps":[[0.61,"Let's start with a detective."],[2.47,"Nobody tells a detective who did it."],[5.06,"She collects small clues."],[6.55,"Mud on the boots."],[8.03,"The dog did not bark."],[9.88,"The back door was unlocked from the inside."],[12.85,"Then she puts the clues together and reaches a conclusion that nobody ever said"],[18.04,"out loud."],[18.78,"The visitor came through the garden, and the dog knew them."],[22.86,"That process, going from things you know to new things that must also be"],[28.05,"true, is called inference."],[29.54,"Today, we teach a computer to do it."]]},{"n":3,"title":"Today’s roadmap","marks":[],"caps":[[1.24,"Here is our path for today."],[3.51,"We start with the idea of a knowledge-based agent,"],[6.92,"an agent that keeps a notebook of facts."],[9.95,"Then we meet the wumpus world, a small cave where we will practise reasoning"],[15.25,"by hand."],[16.01,"Next we learn what logic actually is, and then the simplest logic, propositional logic."],[21.31,"After that we learn two ways to reason with it:"],[25.1,"building proofs, and checking models efficiently."],[27.37,"We finish with agents that use logic over time."],[30.78,"And all the way through, we will return to Northgate Hospital and our patient,"],[36.08,"Maria Santos."]]},{"n":4,"title":"By the end of this lecture, you should be able to…","marks":[],"caps":[[0.78,"Here is what you should be able to do by the end."],[6.01,"One:"],[6.44,"explain what a knowledge base is, and what the operations TELL and ASK do."],[12.54,"Two:"],[12.97,"reason step by step about which squares are safe in the wumpus world."],[18.63,"Three:"],[19.06,"decide whether one sentence entails another, by checking possible worlds."],[23.42,"Four:"],[23.85,"read sentences that use the five logical connectives."],[27.33,"Five:"],[27.77,"build a short proof by hand, including a resolution proof."],[32.12,"And six:"],[32.99,"trace forward chaining, backward chaining, and the DPLL algorithm."],[36.91,"Don't worry if some of those words are new."],[40.83,"Each one gets its own slow introduction."]]},{"n":5,"title":"How this lecture works","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,3.94,3.74],"t":4.68},{"k":"box","g":[4.68,1.43,3.94,3.74],"t":14.79},{"k":"box","g":[8.83,1.43,3.94,3.74],"t":23.29}],"caps":[[0.74,"A quick word on how this lecture works."],[3.98,"First, one step per slide."],[6.0,"Every slide adds exactly one new idea, or one new move in an example,"],[11.66,"and the part that changed is highlighted."],[14.49,"Second, words before symbols."],[16.11,"We will always say an idea in plain English first,"],[20.15,"and only then write it in symbols."],[22.98,"Third, pause and predict."],[24.6,"When you see the pause badge, stop the video and make a guess before"],[30.26,"you continue."],[31.07,"Research on learning is clear:"],[33.09,"a guess, even a wrong one, makes the right answer stick."],[37.54,"We will use two running examples:"],[39.97,"the wumpus cave, and Maria Santos at Northgate Hospital."]]},{"n":6,"title":"Knowledge-Based Agents","marks":[],"caps":[[0.88,"Section seven point one:"],[3.2,"knowledge-based agents."]]},{"n":7,"title":"Two ways to make an agent smart","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,4.74],"t":16.45}],"caps":[[0.63,"There are two broad ways to make an agent smart."],[4.02,"The agents we have built so far were search agents."],[7.4,"The programmer built the rules of one specific problem into them,"],[11.13,"such as the map of Romania, and they found answers by trying sequences of"],[15.87,"actions."],[16.21,"Today's agents are different."],[17.57,"A knowledge-based agent keeps its facts written down as sentences,"],[20.96,"and it finds answers by reasoning from those facts."],[24.0,"And if you want it to know something new, you don't reprogram it."],[28.41,"You just tell it."]]},{"n":8,"title":"The knowledge base: an agent’s notebook","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[8.7,2.66,12.6,2.66],"t":25.84},{"k":"box","g":[0.83,2.13,7.14,1.24],"t":28.25}],"caps":[[0.63,"Here is the central object of this chapter:"],[3.85,"the knowledge base, or K B for short."],[7.06,"Think of it as the agent's notebook."],[9.87,"Each line in the notebook is a sentence, written in a formal language."],[15.1,"The notebook starts empty."],[16.7,"We add sentences with an operation called TELL."],[19.92,"Let's go to Northgate Hospital."],[21.92,"Maria Santos has just arrived in the emergency department."],[25.54,"We TELL the agent our first sentence:"],[28.35,"Maria has difficulty breathing."],[29.96,"That's a fact."]]},{"n":9,"title":"The knowledge base: an agent’s notebook","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.83,3.43,7.14,1.24],"t":4.81}],"caps":[[1.47,"Now we TELL the agent a hospital rule:"],[4.53,"if Maria has difficulty breathing, then she needs an urgent evaluation."],[8.74,"Notice this sentence is different."],[10.66,"It is not a fact about what is happening."],[14.1,"It is an if-then rule."],[16.02,"The arrow means \"implies\", or simply, \"if this, then that\"."]]},{"n":10,"title":"The knowledge base: an agent’s notebook","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.83,4.73,7.14,1.24],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.59,"And one more rule:"],[2.34,"if Maria needs an urgent evaluation, then an emergency physician should see her."],[8.02,"That's our notebook:"],[9.34,"one fact and two rules."],[11.52,"Three sentences."]]},{"n":11,"title":"ASK: putting a question to the knowledge base","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.66,12.6,2.66],"t":1.32},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.93,6.04,3.44],"t":6.22},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,2.93,6.04,3.44],"t":11.12}],"caps":[[0.67,"Now we ASK the agent a question:"],[3.31,"should an emergency physician see Maria?"],[5.57,"Here's the catch."],[6.7,"Look back at the notebook."],[8.58,"No line says that directly."],[10.46,"So the agent cannot just look it up."],[13.48,"It has to work it out from what it does know."],[17.62,"That working-out has a name:"],[19.51,"inference."]]},{"n":12,"title":"Inference: deriving new sentences","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.53,12.24,1.04],"t":17.17}],"caps":[[0.66,"Watch the agent reason, one step at a time."],[4.52,"It starts with the fact:"],[6.67,"Maria has difficulty breathing."],[8.39,"Rule one says, if she has difficulty breathing, then she needs an urgent evaluation."],[14.4,"The if-part is true, so the then-part must be true."],[18.7,"The agent derives a brand-new sentence:"],[21.28,"Maria needs an urgent evaluation."]]},{"n":13,"title":"Inference: deriving new sentences","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.53,12.24,1.04],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.62,"Now it uses that new sentence."],[3.03,"Rule two says, if she needs an urgent evaluation,"],[6.65,"then an emergency physician sees her."],[9.06,"The if-part is now true, so the then-part is true too."],[13.48,"The answer is yes."],[15.08,"The agent reached a conclusion that was never written in its notebook."],[19.9,"That is inference:"],[21.11,"deriving new sentences from old ones."]]},{"n":14,"title":"The knowledge-based agent loop","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[4.83,1.38,3.14,1.34],"t":10.85}],"caps":[[0.65,"Now let's see how a knowledge-based agent runs, step by step."],[4.85,"It repeats a simple loop, once per time step."],[8.28,"Step one:"],[9.04,"it TELLs its knowledge base what it perceives right now."],[12.86,"For example, \"at time zero, I feel a breeze.\""]]},{"n":15,"title":"The knowledge-based agent loop","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[8.33,3.28,3.14,1.34],"t":3.39}],"caps":[[0.61,"Step two:"],[1.43,"it ASKs its knowledge base, \"what action should I take?\" Inference does the work"],[7.19,"here, combining the new percept with everything it already knows."]]},{"n":16,"title":"The knowledge-based agent loop","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[4.83,5.18,3.14,1.34],"t":0.94}],"caps":[[0.67,"Step three:"],[1.4,"it TELLs the knowledge base which action it took, so it remembers."]]},{"n":17,"title":"The knowledge-based agent loop","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[1.33,3.28,3.14,1.34],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.63,"Step four:"],[1.41,"the clock ticks forward by one, and the loop starts again with a new"],[6.86,"percept."],[7.25,"TELL, ASK, TELL, tick."],[8.8,"That's the whole agent program."],[10.75,"All the intelligence lives in the knowledge base and in the inference."]]},{"n":18,"title":"Declarative versus procedural","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,3.54],"t":4.04},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,3.54],"t":12.62}],"caps":[[0.62,"There are two styles of building an agent."],[3.75,"The declarative style writes down what is true,"],[6.87,"such as \"a breeze means a pit is next door\","],[10.77,"and lets inference work out what to do."],[13.89,"The procedural style writes code that says exactly what to do:"],[18.18,"if breeze, mark the neighbours unsafe."],[20.52,"Neither style wins outright."],[22.08,"Practical systems mix them:"],[23.65,"they declare the knowledge, and hand-code the parts that must run fast."]]},{"n":19,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.28,3.973,2.44],"t":17.07},{"k":"box","g":[4.663,3.28,3.973,2.44],"t":17.07}],"caps":[[0.67,"Pause and predict."],[2.27,"A hospital agent receives a new reading:"],[5.99,"Maria's oxygen level is low."],[8.65,"Then someone asks it:"],[10.78,"does Maria need monitoring?"],[12.91,"Which operations happen, and in what order?"],[16.63,"A:"],[17.17,"ask, then tell."],[18.76,"B:"],[19.29,"tell, then ask."],[20.89,"C:"],[21.42,"only ask."],[22.48,"Pause the video, and choose."]]},{"n":20,"title":"Answer: TELL, then ASK","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.63,12.24,1.14],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.13,12.24,1.74],"t":14.67}],"caps":[[0.65,"The answer is B:"],[2.27,"tell, then ask."],[3.48,"First the agent TELLs its knowledge base the new reading:"],[7.51,"Maria's oxygen is low."],[9.12,"Then it ASKs the question, and inference uses every fact, old and new,"],[14.37,"to answer."],[15.17,"Why not C?"],[16.38,"Without the TELL, the agent would be answering with old information."],[20.82,"It has to hear the new fact first."]]},{"n":21,"title":"Recap · 7.1","marks":[],"caps":[[0.59,"Let's recap section seven point one."],[3.09,"A knowledge base is a notebook of sentences:"],[6.43,"facts and if-then rules."],[8.1,"TELL adds a sentence."],[9.77,"ASK poses a question."],[11.43,"And inference derives new sentences that must be true from the ones we already"],[17.27,"have."],[17.69,"Next, a cave where we can practise all of this."]]}]},{"id":"ch7-s2","title":"7.2 The wumpus world","dir":"ch7/s2","slides":[{"n":22,"title":"The Wumpus World","marks":[],"caps":[[0.7,"Section seven point two:"],[2.17,"the wumpus world."],[3.28,"This little cave is the textbook's favourite example,"],[6.24,"and it is perfect for learning to reason,"],[9.2,"because every conclusion can be checked with your own eyes."]]},{"n":23,"title":"The cave: a 4 × 4 grid of rooms","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.93,1.33,5.84,3.54],"t":5.43}],"caps":[[0.62,"Here is the cave:"],[2.14,"a grid of sixteen rooms, four by four."],[5.19,"The agent starts in the bottom-left room."],[7.86,"We name each room by its column and its row,"],[11.67,"so the start is square one, one."],[14.34,"The square to its right is two, one:"],[17.39,"column two, row one."],[18.91,"The square above the start is one, two."],[21.96,"Take a moment to find square three, one."],[25.01,"It's column three, bottom row."]]},{"n":24,"title":"Danger 1: the wumpus","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.93,1.33,5.84,3.54],"t":2.66}],"caps":[[0.71,"Somewhere in the cave lives the wumpus, a beast that eats anyone who walks"],[5.49,"into its room."],[6.52,"In this cave it is in square one, three."],[9.59,"The agent has exactly one arrow, which it can shoot in a straight line"],[14.38,"to kill the wumpus."]]},{"n":25,"title":"Danger 2: bottomless pits","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.93,1.33,5.84,3.54],"t":2.07}],"caps":[[0.9,"Second danger:"],[1.74,"bottomless pits."],[2.59,"Any room except the start might have one."],[5.99,"Walk into a pit and the game is over."],[9.81,"Here there are three pits:"],[11.93,"in three, one; three, three; and four, four."]]},{"n":26,"title":"The prize: the gold","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.93,1.33,5.84,3.54],"t":5.46}],"caps":[[0.64,"And somewhere is the prize:"],[2.53,"a heap of gold, here in square two, three."],[5.94,"The agent's goal is to find the gold, grab it,"],[9.72,"get back to square one, one, and climb out alive."]]},{"n":27,"title":"Percept 1: a breeze means a pit is next door","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.93,1.33,5.84,4.74],"t":9.43}],"caps":[[1.04,"The agent can't see, but it can sense."],[4.0,"It gets five kinds of percept."],[6.21,"Let's take them one at a time."],[8.79,"First, the breeze."],[9.9,"In any room directly next to a pit, the agent feels a breeze."],[14.7,"Directly next to means up, down, left, or right."],[18.02,"Never diagonal."],[18.76,"Look at square two, one:"],[20.6,"it's next to the pit in three, one, so it's breezy."],[24.66,"Here's the key thing to remember:"],[26.88,"a breeze tells you a pit is next door, but not which door."]]},{"n":28,"title":"Percept 2: a stench means the wumpus is next door","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.93,1.33,5.84,4.74],"t":1.71}],"caps":[[0.67,"Second percept:"],[1.43,"the stench."],[2.19,"In a room directly next to the wumpus, the agent smells it."],[6.76,"The wumpus is in one, three, so the stench is in one,"],[11.33,"two below it, one, four above it, and two, three to its right."],[16.29,"Same idea as the breeze:"],[18.19,"a stench means the wumpus is next door,"],[21.24,"but you don't yet know which door."]]},{"n":29,"title":"Percepts 3–5, actions, and the score","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,3.99,2.34],"t":1.77},{"k":"box","g":[4.65,1.43,3.99,2.34],"t":4.42},{"k":"box","g":[8.78,1.43,3.99,2.34],"t":8.21},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.93,6.04,2.54],"t":12.37},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,3.93,6.04,2.54],"t":16.16}],"caps":[[0.74,"Three more percepts."],[1.87,"Glitter, in the room with the gold."],[4.52,"Bump, when the agent walks into a wall."],[7.55,"And a scream, heard everywhere, when the wumpus dies."],[10.96,"The agent can move forward, turn left or right, grab, shoot its arrow,"],[15.88,"and climb out from the start square."],[18.53,"The score:"],[19.29,"plus one thousand for climbing out with the gold,"],[22.7,"minus one thousand for dying, minus one for each action,"],[26.48,"and minus ten for using the arrow."],[29.13,"So the agent wants to be safe, and also efficient."]]},{"n":30,"title":"What the agent actually knows at the start","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.93,1.33,5.84,2.54],"t":1.07},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,3.98,5.84,2.14],"t":13.09}],"caps":[[1.17,"Now forget the true cave."],[2.99,"From here on, the grid shows only what the agent knows."],[7.0,"At the start, that's almost nothing:"],[9.19,"it's in square one, one, and everything else is fog."],[12.83,"Its only weapon is reasoning from its percepts."],[15.75,"Let's watch it think, one step at a time."]]},{"n":31,"title":"Reasoning in the wumpus world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[0.86,5.05,1.53,1.45],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,3.88,5.84,2.24],"t":5.44},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,2.28,5.84,1.49],"t":12.93}],"caps":[[0.96,"Step one."],[1.79,"The agent is in square one, one, and it's alive."],[5.96,"So this square has no pit and no wumpus."],[9.7,"We mark it O K, meaning safe."],[12.61,"Its percept here is:"],[14.28,"nothing."],[14.69,"No breeze."],[15.53,"No stench."],[16.36,"Hold on to that."]]},{"n":32,"title":"Reasoning in the wumpus world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[0.86,3.8,1.53,1.45],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[2.11,5.05,1.53,1.45],"t":1.5},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,2.28,5.84,1.49],"t":5.23},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,3.88,5.84,2.24],"t":11.89}],"caps":[[0.53,"Step two."],[1.27,"Which squares are next to one, one?"],[3.85,"Only two:"],[4.59,"one, two above it, and two, one to its right."],[8.29,"The other sides are walls."],[10.14,"If either neighbour held a pit, the agent would feel a breeze."],[14.57,"If either held the wumpus, it would smell a stench."]]},{"n":33,"title":"Reasoning in the wumpus world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[0.86,3.8,1.53,1.45],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[2.11,5.05,1.53,1.45],"t":1.5},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,2.28,5.84,1.49],"t":2.67},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,3.88,5.84,2.24],"t":16.54}],"caps":[[0.79,"Step three."],[1.58,"It felt no breeze, so neither neighbour has a pit."],[5.54,"It smelled no stench, so neither neighbour has the wumpus."],[9.51,"Conclusion:"],[9.9,"one, two and two, one are both safe."],[13.07,"Mark them O K."],[14.66,"Notice what just happened."],[16.24,"Nobody told the agent these squares were safe."],[19.41,"It deduced it, purely from the absence of a breeze and a smell."]]},{"n":34,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.28,3.973,2.44],"t":6.75}],"caps":[[0.39,"Pause and predict."],[1.88,"The agent knows one, two and two, one are both safe,"],[7.34,"and it hasn't visited either."],[9.83,"It needs more information, so it must move."],[13.8,"What's a reasonable choice?"],[15.79,"A:"],[16.29,"either safe square."],[17.78,"B:"],[18.28,"two, two, because it's in the middle."],[21.76,"C:"],[22.25,"stay put."],[23.25,"Pause, and decide."]]},{"n":35,"title":"Reasoning in the wumpus world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.11,5.05,1.53,1.45],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,3.88,5.84,2.24],"t":15.93}],"caps":[[0.65,"The answer is A."],[2.05,"Either safe square is a good choice, and the middle square is not proven"],[6.94,"safe."],[7.29,"Our agent moves right, into two, one."],[9.74,"One, one is now marked V, for visited."],[12.54,"In two, one, it feels a breeze!"],[14.98,"So there's a pit in at least one neighbour of two, one."],[19.18,"The question is:"],[20.23,"which one?"]]},{"n":36,"title":"Reasoning in the wumpus world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.11,3.8,1.53,1.45],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[3.36,5.05,1.53,1.45],"t":1.5},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,2.28,5.84,1.49],"t":3.28},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,3.88,5.84,2.24],"t":10.48}],"caps":[[0.85,"Step five:"],[1.69,"list the suspects."],[2.96,"The neighbours of two, one are one, one, two, two, and three, one."],[8.46,"One, one can't have a pit; we were just standing there."],[13.12,"So the pit is in two, two, or three, one, or possibly both."],[18.62,"We mark both with P question mark:"],[21.59,"maybe a pit."]]},{"n":37,"title":"Reasoning in the wumpus world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.11,3.8,1.53,1.45],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[3.36,5.05,1.53,1.45],"t":1.5}],"caps":[[0.95,"Step six:"],[1.74,"notice what is missing."],[3.31,"There's no stench in two, one."],[5.68,"So the wumpus is not in any neighbour of two, one."],[10.01,"In particular, it is not in two, two, and not in three, one."],[15.13,"Remember that fact."],[16.31,"It's going to matter very soon."]]},{"n":38,"title":"Reasoning in the wumpus world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[0.86,3.8,1.53,1.45],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,3.88,5.84,2.24],"t":3.16},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,2.28,5.84,1.49],"t":3.56}],"caps":[[0.75,"Step seven."],[1.65,"From two, one, the only unexplored neighbours are maybe-pits."],[5.67,"A cautious agent only enters squares it knows are safe."],[10.14,"Which safe square hasn't it visited?"],[12.82,"One, two."],[13.72,"So it turns around, goes back through one, one, and up into one, two."]]},{"n":39,"title":"Reasoning in the wumpus world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[0.86,3.8,1.53,1.45],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,2.28,5.84,1.49],"t":4.2},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,3.88,5.84,2.24],"t":6.48}],"caps":[[0.88,"Step eight."],[1.64,"In one, two, the agent smells a stench, but feels no breeze."],[6.2,"Two clues at once."],[7.72,"Let's use them one at a time, starting with the stench."]]},{"n":40,"title":"Reasoning in the wumpus world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[0.86,5.05,1.53,1.45],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[0.86,2.55,1.53,1.45],"t":1.5},{"k":"ring","g":[2.11,3.8,1.53,1.45],"t":2.4},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,2.28,5.84,1.49],"t":2.92}],"caps":[[0.76,"Step nine."],[1.51,"The stench means the wumpus is in a neighbour of one, two."],[6.04,"Those are one, one; two, two; and one, three."],[9.43,"Not one, one."],[10.56,"We've been there and we're still alive."],[13.2,"That leaves two, two or one, three."]]},{"n":41,"title":"Reasoning in the wumpus world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[0.86,2.55,1.53,1.45],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[2.11,3.8,1.53,1.45],"t":1.5},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,2.28,5.84,1.49],"t":4.22},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,3.88,5.84,2.24],"t":8.98}],"caps":[[1.14,"Step ten."],[1.94,"Now we use the fact from step six."],[5.11,"Back in two, one, there was no stench,"],[8.28,"so the wumpus is not in two, two."],[11.46,"Only one suspect remains:"],[13.04,"one, three."],[13.84,"The wumpus must be there."],[15.82,"We mark it W with an exclamation mark:"],[18.99,"certain."],[19.39,"That's the power of logic:"],[21.37,"a clue from one square, combined with a clue from a different square,"],[26.53,"at a different time."]]},{"n":42,"title":"Reasoning in the wumpus world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.11,3.8,1.53,1.45],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,2.28,5.84,1.49],"t":2.55},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,3.88,5.84,2.24],"t":6.07}],"caps":[[0.69,"Step eleven."],[1.47,"Now the second clue:"],[3.04,"there's no breeze in one, two."],[5.39,"So no neighbour of one, two has a pit."],[8.91,"That includes two, two."],[10.47,"Two, two has no pit."]]},{"n":43,"title":"Reasoning in the wumpus world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[3.36,5.05,1.53,1.45],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,2.28,5.84,1.49],"t":0.56},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,3.88,5.84,2.24],"t":5.25}],"caps":[[0.66,"Step twelve."],[1.44,"Step five said the pit is in two, two or three, one."],[6.13,"We just ruled out two, two."],[8.47,"So the pit must be in three, one."],[11.59,"P exclamation mark:"],[12.76,"certain."]]},{"n":44,"title":"Reasoning in the wumpus world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.11,3.8,1.53,1.45],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,2.28,5.84,1.49],"t":0.93},{"k":"box","g":[6.93,3.88,5.84,2.24],"t":9.85}],"caps":[[1.03,"Step thirteen."],[1.81,"Two, two has no pit, from step eleven, and no wumpus, from step six."],[7.24,"So two, two is safe, and the agent can move there with complete confidence."],[12.67,"Look at the grid."],[14.22,"Every mark on it was proved."],[16.54,"The agent never took a risk."]]},{"n":45,"title":"What the wumpus world teaches","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,3.99,4.54],"t":3.13},{"k":"box","g":[4.65,1.43,3.99,4.54],"t":9.8},{"k":"box","g":[8.78,1.43,3.99,4.54],"t":19.39}],"caps":[[0.73,"Three lessons from the cave."],[2.82,"One:"],[3.23,"absence is a clue."],[4.9,"\"No breeze\" told us just as much as a breeze did."],[9.49,"Two:"],[9.9,"combine clues across time and place."],[12.41,"We found the wumpus by joining a clue from two,"],[16.57,"one with a clue from one, two."],[19.49,"Three:"],[19.91,"certain, not lucky."],[21.16,"Every conclusion was guaranteed."],[22.83,"If the clues are correct, the conclusion is correct."],[26.58,"The rest of this chapter turns this kind of careful reasoning into something a"],[32.42,"computer can do mechanically."]]},{"n":46,"title":"Recap · 7.2","marks":[],"caps":[[0.73,"Recap of section seven point two."],[3.18,"The cave has pits, one wumpus, and gold,"],[6.44,"and the agent only senses its own square."],[9.7,"A breeze or a stench means danger in some neighbouring square."],[14.18,"Safe squares are proved from percepts, including absent ones."],[17.85,"And inference combines clues from different times and places."],[21.52,"Next question:"],[22.33,"what exactly does it mean for a conclusion to \"follow\" from what we know?"],[28.04,"That's logic."]]}]},{"id":"ch7-s3","title":"7.3 Logic: models and entailment","dir":"ch7/s3","slides":[{"n":47,"title":"Logic","marks":[],"caps":[[0.68,"Section seven point three:"],[2.27,"logic."],[2.67,"This is the most important section of the chapter."],[6.26,"If you understand the idea of possible worlds here,"],[9.85,"everything after it gets much easier."]]},{"n":48,"title":"Syntax: the grammar of sentences","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,2.34],"t":9.54},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.93,12.24,2.14],"t":24.17}],"caps":[[0.62,"Every logic has two parts."],[2.81,"The first is syntax:"],[4.56,"the grammar."],[5.44,"You already know syntax from arithmetic."],[8.07,"\"x plus y equals four\" is a well-formed sentence."],[12.01,"\"x four y plus equals\" is not; it's just a jumble."],[16.83,"Logic is the same."],[18.58,"\"B implies U\" is well formed."],[21.21,"\"Implies B U and\" is a jumble."],[24.27,"Syntax only tells us which strings count as sentences."],[28.22,"It says nothing yet about true or false."]]},{"n":49,"title":"Semantics: what makes a sentence true","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,2.44],"t":4.84},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,2.44],"t":4.84},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.03,12.24,2.04],"t":17.75}],"caps":[[0.76,"The second part is semantics:"],[2.66,"meaning."],[3.04,"Is \"x plus y equals four\" true?"],[5.7,"It depends on the world."],[7.6,"In a world where x is two and y is two, it's true."],[12.53,"In a world where x is one and y is one, it's false."],[17.47,"So semantics answers:"],[18.61,"in each possible world, is this sentence true or false?"],[22.4,"The same sentence can be true in one world and false in another."],[27.34,"That idea, possible worlds, is the key to the whole chapter."]]},{"n":50,"title":"A model is one possible world","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.14,2.14],"t":3.14},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.73,6.14,2.44],"t":11.22}],"caps":[[0.69,"Let's make \"possible world\" precise, with Maria."],[3.66,"We use two symbols."],[5.37,"B means Maria has difficulty breathing."],[7.92,"U means Maria needs an urgent evaluation."],[10.9,"A model is one complete way the world could be:"],[15.15,"every symbol gets a value, true or false, with nothing left open."],[20.25,"Here's one model:"],[21.53,"B is true, U is false."],[24.08,"In this world, Maria has difficulty breathing, but does not need an urgent evaluation."],[30.04,"Maybe that's a strange world, but it is a possible combination."]]},{"n":51,"title":"Two symbols give four possible worlds","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,5.96,6.4,5.96],"t":12.79},{"k":"ul","g":[6.9,5.96,12.6,5.96],"t":24.17}],"caps":[[0.68,"How many possible worlds are there?"],[3.21,"Each symbol can be true or false."],[6.15,"B has two choices, and for each of those, U has two choices."],[11.63,"Two times two:"],[12.89,"four worlds."],[13.74,"Here they are."],[15.0,"World one:"],[15.84,"both false."],[16.69,"World two:"],[17.53,"only U true."],[18.79,"World three:"],[19.63,"only B true."],[20.9,"World four:"],[21.74,"both true."],[22.58,"In general, with n symbols, there are two to the power n worlds."],[28.06,"Keep that number in mind; it will come back to bite us."]]},{"n":52,"title":"A sentence picks out the worlds where it is true","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,5.86,12.6,5.86],"t":6.87}],"caps":[[0.68,"Here's a new way to think about a sentence."],[3.82,"A sentence picks out the worlds where it is true."],[7.32,"Take the sentence B:"],[8.71,"Maria has difficulty breathing."],[10.11,"Which of the four worlds make it true?"],[12.91,"Worlds three and four, where B is T."],[15.7,"In worlds one and two, it's false, so they're greyed out."],[19.55,"So the meaning of a sentence is just a set of worlds."]]},{"n":53,"title":"Which worlds does the rule  B ⇒ U  allow?","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.33,12.24,1.84],"t":10.41},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,2.977,2.74],"t":18.92},{"k":"box","g":[6.705,1.43,2.977,2.74],"t":18.92},{"k":"box","g":[9.793,1.43,2.977,2.74],"t":32.3}],"caps":[[0.78,"Now the rule:"],[2.0,"B implies U."],[3.22,"If Maria has difficulty breathing, she needs an urgent evaluation."],[7.27,"Which worlds does it allow?"],[9.3,"Think of the rule as a promise."],[12.13,"When is a promise broken?"],[14.16,"Only when the \"if\" part happens, and the \"then\" part doesn't."],[18.62,"That's world three:"],[19.83,"B true, U false."],[21.45,"The rule is broken there."],[23.48,"In worlds one and two, B is false."],[26.72,"The rule makes no promise about that case, so it isn't broken."],[31.59,"And in world four, both are true, so the promise is kept."],[36.45,"So B implies U is true in worlds one, two, and four."]]},{"n":54,"title":"Which worlds fit everything in the KB?","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.28,12.24,1.09],"t":4.5},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.36,12.6,2.36],"t":10.79}],"caps":[[0.67,"Now the whole knowledge base."],[2.64,"It contains two sentences:"],[4.21,"the fact B, and the rule B implies U."],[7.75,"A world fits the knowledge base only if both sentences are true in it."],[13.25,"Let's check the worlds one at a time."],[16.39,"World one:"],[17.18,"B is false."],[18.35,"But the knowledge base says B."],[20.71,"So world one is ruled out."]]},{"n":55,"title":"Which worlds fit everything in the KB?","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.28,12.24,1.09],"t":0.48}],"caps":[[0.58,"World two:"],[1.38,"B is false again."],[2.97,"Ruled out, for the same reason."]]},{"n":56,"title":"Which worlds fit everything in the KB?","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.28,12.24,1.09],"t":0.61}],"caps":[[0.71,"World three:"],[1.62,"B is true, good."],[3.44,"But U is false, so the rule B implies U is broken."],[8.89,"Ruled out."]]},{"n":57,"title":"Which worlds fit everything in the KB?","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[9.793,2.53,2.977,2.64],"t":0.78},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.28,12.24,1.09],"t":0.78},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.36,12.6,2.36],"t":2.37}],"caps":[[0.88,"World four:"],[1.67,"B is true, and U is true, so the rule is kept."],[6.45,"Both sentences are true."],[8.05,"World four fits the knowledge base."],[10.43,"Out of four possible worlds, only one is consistent with everything the agent knows."]]},{"n":58,"title":"Entailment: true in every world the KB allows","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.13,6.74,2.14],"t":7.0},{"k":"box","g":[9.793,1.43,2.977,2.54],"t":14.83}],"caps":[[0.76,"Now we can answer a question:"],[3.0,"does Maria need an urgent evaluation?"],[5.23,"Here's the test."],[6.35,"Look at every world that fits the knowledge base, and ask:"],[10.45,"is U true in all of them?"],[13.06,"There is only one such world, world four, and U is true there."],[17.91,"So yes."],[18.65,"When a sentence is true in every world where the knowledge base is true,"],[23.87,"we say the knowledge base entails it."],[26.48,"We write K B, then this symbol, a turnstile with two bars, then U."],[31.7,"Read it as:"],[32.82,"K B entails U."],[34.31,"It means U must be true, given what we know."]]},{"n":59,"title":"Entailment in one picture","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.83,1.43,5.54,4.74],"t":4.38},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,2.34],"t":8.88}],"caps":[[0.66,"Here's entailment as a picture."],[2.4,"The big circle is all the worlds where U is true."],[6.21,"The small dark circle is all the worlds where the knowledge base is true."],[11.06,"Entailment means the small circle sits entirely inside the big one."],[14.87,"Wherever the knowledge base is true, U is true too."],[18.34,"The textbook writes it with set notation."],[20.77,"M of alpha means the set of models, the worlds, where alpha is true."],[25.62,"And K B entails alpha exactly when M of K B is a subset"],[30.47,"of M of alpha."],[31.86,"Now that you know the picture, that formula is just a short way to"],[36.71,"say \"the small circle is inside the big one\"."]]},{"n":60,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[],"caps":[[0.6,"Pause and predict."],[2.1,"New knowledge base:"],[3.59,"the rule B implies U, and the fact U."],[8.09,"Maria did get an urgent evaluation."],[11.09,"Does this knowledge base entail B, that she had difficulty breathing?"],[16.58,"A:"],[17.08,"yes."],[17.58,"B:"],[18.08,"no."],[18.58,"C:"],[19.08,"we can't tell."],[20.58,"Check the four worlds yourself, then continue."]]},{"n":61,"title":"Answer: No — check the worlds","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[3.618,1.43,2.977,2.74],"t":2.46},{"k":"box","g":[9.793,1.43,2.977,2.74],"t":4.73},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,4.33,6.04,1.74],"t":9.64}],"caps":[[0.67,"The answer is B:"],[2.18,"no."],[2.56,"Two worlds fit this knowledge base:"],[4.83,"world two and world four."],[6.72,"In world four, B is true."],[8.99,"But in world two, B is false:"],[11.63,"Maria got an urgent evaluation for some other reason, perhaps a high fever."],[16.55,"One world where the knowledge base is true but B is false is enough."],[21.84,"That world is called a counterexample."],[24.11,"So the knowledge base does not entail B."],[27.14,"We write the turnstile with a slash through it."]]},{"n":62,"title":"A classic trap: affirming the consequent","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":11.51}],"caps":[[1.01,"This mistake is so common it has a name:"],[5.16,"affirming the consequent."],[6.54,"From \"B implies U\" and \"U\", people conclude \"B\"."],[10.69,"It's invalid."],[11.61,"Everyday version:"],[12.54,"if it rains, the grass is wet."],[15.76,"The grass is wet."],[17.61,"So it rained?"],[18.99,"No."],[19.45,"The sprinkler might be on."],[21.75,"The safe direction is forward:"],[24.06,"from \"B implies U\" and \"B\", you can conclude U."],[28.67,"Rules run forward, not backward."]]},{"n":63,"title":"Back to the cave: 8 possible pit arrangements","marks":[],"caps":[[0.78,"Let's use possible worlds on the cave."],[3.99,"After visiting one, one and two, one, three squares are uncertain:"],[9.04,"one, two; two, two; and three, one."],[12.26,"Each could have a pit or not."],[15.47,"Two times two times two:"],[17.76,"eight possible worlds."],[19.14,"Here they are, every combination."]]},{"n":64,"title":"Keep only the worlds that fit the percepts","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[3.618,1.93,2.977,2.165],"t":10.66},{"k":"box","g":[6.705,1.93,2.977,2.165],"t":11.78}],"caps":[[0.7,"Now apply what the agent knows."],[2.93,"No breeze in one, one means no pit in one, two."],[7.03,"And the breeze in two, one means a pit in two,"],[11.13,"two or three, one, or both."],[13.37,"Cross out every world that breaks either fact."],[16.35,"3 worlds survive."],[17.47,"These are the worlds consistent with the knowledge base."]]},{"n":65,"title":"Is “no pit in [1,2]” entailed?","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,4.007,3.04],"t":12.86},{"k":"box","g":[4.647,1.43,4.007,3.04],"t":13.22}],"caps":[[0.84,"Question one:"],[1.57,"is there no pit in one, two?"],[4.14,"Look at the three surviving worlds."],[6.35,"In every one of them, one, two has no pit."],[10.02,"So the knowledge base entails \"no pit in one, two\"."],[13.69,"That square is safe, proved by checking every possible world."]]},{"n":66,"title":"Is “no pit in [2,2]” entailed?","marks":[],"caps":[[0.99,"Question two:"],[1.7,"is there no pit in two, two?"],[4.19,"Now look:"],[4.9,"in 2 of the three worlds, two, two does have a pit."],[9.16,"So \"no pit in two, two\" is not entailed."],[12.36,"That doesn't mean there is a pit there; it means the agent doesn't know"],[17.34,"yet."],[17.69,"This is exactly the \"P question mark\" we drew by hand earlier."],[21.96,"This method, listing every world and checking, is called model checking."]]},{"n":67,"title":"Sound and complete: two promises an inference method can make","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,3.14],"t":7.95},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,3.14],"t":11.65},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.73,12.24,1.44],"t":19.05}],"caps":[[1.06,"Model checking works, but we'll soon want other methods."],[4.76,"To compare them, we ask for two promises."],[8.05,"Sound:"],[8.46,"the method only derives sentences that really are entailed."],[12.16,"It never gives a false alarm."],[14.62,"Complete:"],[15.04,"the method can derive every sentence that is entailed."],[18.74,"It never misses one."],[20.38,"Think of a smoke alarm."],[22.44,"A sound alarm never beeps unless there's smoke."],[25.73,"A complete alarm beeps every time there is smoke."],[29.43,"We want both."],[30.66,"Model checking, by the way, is both sound and complete."],[34.77,"Its problem is speed, which we'll see soon."]]},{"n":68,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.28,3.973,2.44],"t":9.51},{"k":"box","g":[4.663,3.28,3.973,2.44],"t":9.51},{"k":"box","g":[8.797,3.28,3.973,2.44],"t":9.51}],"caps":[[0.64,"Pause and predict."],[2.06,"Imagine a lazy inference method that answers every question with \"I can't conclude anything\"."],[8.67,"Is it sound?"],[10.09,"Is it complete?"],[11.5,"Pause, and decide."]]},{"n":69,"title":"Answer: sound, but not complete","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,3.14],"t":2.27},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,3.14],"t":9.75},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.73,12.24,1.44],"t":12.25}],"caps":[[0.7,"The answer is B:"],[2.37,"sound, but not complete."],[4.03,"It never says anything false, because it never says anything at all."],[9.02,"But it misses everything that is entailed."],[11.93,"So soundness by itself is easy."],[14.43,"The challenge is being sound, complete, and fast."]]},{"n":70,"title":"Recap · 7.3","marks":[],"caps":[[0.72,"Recap of section seven point three."],[3.27,"Syntax says which strings are sentences; semantics says whether they're true in a world."],[9.23,"A model is one possible world, and n symbols give two to the n"],[15.19,"models."],[15.62,"K B entails alpha when alpha is true in every world where K B"],[21.57,"is true; a single counterexample breaks it."],[24.55,"And model checking means listing every world and checking."],[28.39,"Next, we look closely at the language itself:"],[31.79,"propositional logic."]]}]},{"id":"ch7-s4","title":"7.4 Propositional logic","dir":"ch7/s4","slides":[{"n":71,"title":"Propositional Logic","marks":[],"caps":[[0.72,"Section seven point four:"],[2.44,"propositional logic, the simplest useful logic."],[5.01,"We'll learn its symbols and its five connectives, one at a time."]]},{"n":72,"title":"Atoms: symbols that are simply true or false","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,4.74],"t":3.45},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,2.24],"t":26.24},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,3.83,6.04,2.34],"t":30.38}],"caps":[[0.65,"The building blocks of propositional logic are proposition symbols, also called atoms."],[5.63,"Think of each one as a light switch:"],[8.94,"on means true, off means false."],[11.43,"P one two means \"there is a pit in square one, two\"."],[16.4,"B two one means \"there's a breeze in two, one\"."],[20.54,"And for Maria, U means \"she needs an urgent evaluation\"."],[24.68,"There are also two fixed symbols:"],[27.17,"True, always on, and False, always off."],[30.07,"A single symbol by itself is an atomic sentence."],[33.8,"Everything else is built by joining atoms with connectives."],[37.52,"There are five connectives."],[39.18,"Let's meet them one at a time."]]},{"n":73,"title":"Connective 1 of 5:  ¬  (not)","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.86,7.1,2.86],"t":5.78},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.03,6.74,1.74],"t":10.01}],"caps":[[0.81,"Connective one:"],[1.66,"not."],[2.08,"It's written with this hook symbol."],[4.61,"Not A is true exactly when A is false."],[8.42,"It flips the switch."],[10.11,"Example:"],[10.53,"not P one two means \"there is no pit in one, two\"."],[15.6,"Look at the little table:"],[17.71,"A false gives not A true; A true gives not A false."]]},{"n":74,"title":"Connective 2 of 5:  ∧  (and)","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.86,7.1,2.86],"t":6.09},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.03,6.74,1.74],"t":11.56}],"caps":[[0.72,"Connective two:"],[1.63,"and, written like an upside-down V."],[4.37,"A and B is true only when both are true."],[8.93,"One true row out of four."],[11.66,"Example:"],[12.12,"Maria has breathing trouble and low oxygen."],[15.31,"Both have to hold."]]},{"n":75,"title":"Connective 3 of 5:  ∨  (or)","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.86,7.1,2.86],"t":5.09},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.03,6.74,1.74],"t":16.46}],"caps":[[0.72,"Connective three:"],[1.53,"or, written like a V."],[3.56,"A or B is true when at least one of them is true."],[8.84,"Careful:"],[9.25,"in logic, \"or\" includes the case where both are true."],[13.31,"It's not \"one or the other but not both\"."],[16.96,"Example:"],[17.37,"a pit in two, two or three, one, or both."],[21.43,"That's exactly what the breeze in two, one told us."]]},{"n":76,"title":"Connective 4 of 5:  ⇒  (implies)","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.86,7.1,2.86],"t":8.09}],"caps":[[0.78,"Connective four:"],[1.6,"implies, the arrow."],[2.84,"A implies B."],[4.07,"A is called the premise, and B the conclusion."],[7.78,"It's false in only one row:"],[10.25,"A true, B false."],[11.89,"You saw this already with Maria's rule."],[14.77,"The next slide explains why the other rows are true."]]},{"n":77,"title":"Connective 5 of 5:  ⇔  (if and only if)","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.86,7.1,2.86],"t":7.19},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.03,6.74,1.74],"t":13.0}],"caps":[[0.66,"Connective five:"],[1.49,"if and only if, the double arrow."],[4.39,"A if and only if B is true when A and B have the"],[10.2,"same value:"],[11.03,"both true, or both false."],[13.1,"Example:"],[13.52,"square one, one is breezy if and only if a neighbour has a pit."],[19.32,"Breeze and pit-next-door always go together."],[21.81,"It's really two arrows at once:"],[24.3,"one each way."]]},{"n":78,"title":"Why is  A ⇒ B  true when A is false?  Think of a promise","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.41,12.6,2.41],"t":6.99},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.58,6.04,1.74],"t":11.72},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,2.58,6.04,1.74],"t":13.69},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,4.43,6.04,1.74],"t":16.85},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.43,6.04,1.74],"t":18.43}],"caps":[[1.17,"Why is \"A implies B\" true when A is false?"],[5.11,"Think of it as a promise from your manager:"],[8.66,"if you finish your project, you get a bonus."],[12.21,"Finished, and got the bonus:"],[14.19,"promise kept."],[14.98,"Finished, but no bonus:"],[16.55,"promise broken."],[17.34,"That's the only false row."],[19.32,"Now the two cases where you didn't finish."],[22.47,"Got a bonus anyway?"],[24.05,"The manager was generous, but didn't break the promise."],[27.6,"No bonus?"],[28.39,"Also fine."],[29.18,"The promise only talked about what happens if you finish."],[33.12,"So an implication is false only when the premise is true and the conclusion"],[38.64,"is false."]]},{"n":79,"title":"Two things ⇒ does NOT mean","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,4.64],"t":6.49},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,4.64],"t":22.78}],"caps":[[1.45,"Two things the arrow does not mean."],[4.45,"First, it does not mean causation."],[7.02,"\"Five is odd implies Tokyo is in Japan\" is a true sentence in logic,"],[13.02,"because both parts are true, even though one has nothing to do with the"],[19.02,"other."],[19.45,"Logic only checks truth values."],[21.6,"Second, it's not reversible."],[23.31,"A implies B does not give you B implies A."],[27.6,"\"Pneumonia implies a respiratory evaluation\" does not mean \"an evaluation implies pneumonia\"."],[32.74,"That's the affirming-the-consequent trap again."]]},{"n":80,"title":"Order of operations, like PEMDAS","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,4.56,12.6,4.56],"t":11.51},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.73,12.24,1.34],"t":17.81}],"caps":[[0.99,"Like PEMDAS in arithmetic, connectives have an order."],[4.14,"Not binds tightest, then and, then or, then implies, and finally if-and-only-if."],[8.86,"So \"not A and B implies C\" means:"],[12.01,"first not A, then and it with B, then the whole thing implies C."],[17.51,"Honestly, when in doubt, add parentheses."],[19.87,"Nobody minds extra parentheses."]]},{"n":81,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.28,3.973,2.44],"t":5.73},{"k":"box","g":[4.663,3.28,3.973,2.44],"t":8.08}],"caps":[[0.55,"Pause and predict."],[2.31,"In the world where B is true and U is false,"],[8.76,"what is the value of \"not B, or U\"?"],[14.05,"Work it out from the inside."]]},{"n":82,"title":"Answer: False — evaluate from the inside out","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.43,12.24,0.99],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.83,12.24,1.34],"t":12.59}],"caps":[[0.63,"The answer is false."],[2.24,"Go from the inside out."],[4.25,"B is true, so not B is false."],[7.47,"Then false or U, and U is false, so false or false is false."],[13.1,"Notice something:"],[13.9,"not B or U is false in exactly the same world where B implies"],[19.53,"U is false."],[20.74,"Hold that thought; it will matter soon."]]},{"n":83,"title":"Writing the wumpus knowledge base","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,12.24,0.92],"t":11.47}],"caps":[[0.78,"Now let's write a knowledge base for the cave, one sentence at a time."],[5.23,"Just the part about pits and breezes in the squares we've seen."],[9.04,"R one:"],[9.67,"not P one one."],[10.94,"There's no pit in the start square."]]},{"n":84,"title":"Writing the wumpus knowledge base","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.31,12.24,0.92],"t":7.35}],"caps":[[0.69,"R two:"],[1.37,"B one one if and only if, P one two or P two one."],[6.1,"Square one, one is breezy exactly when one of its neighbours has a pit."],[10.83,"This is a rule about how the cave works."]]},{"n":85,"title":"Writing the wumpus knowledge base","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.19,12.24,0.92],"t":0.99}],"caps":[[0.7,"R three:"],[1.47,"the same rule for square two, one."],[4.14,"B two one if and only if P one one,"],[7.95,"or P two two, or P three one."]]},{"n":86,"title":"Writing the wumpus knowledge base","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.07,12.24,0.92],"t":2.23}],"caps":[[0.67,"Now the percepts."],[1.91,"R four:"],[2.75,"not B one one."],[4.41,"The agent felt no breeze in one, one."]]},{"n":87,"title":"Writing the wumpus knowledge base","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.95,12.24,0.92],"t":1.01}],"caps":[[0.68,"R five:"],[1.55,"B two one."],[2.87,"The agent felt a breeze in two, one."],[6.37,"Five sentences:"],[7.25,"three rules about how caves work, and two percepts."],[11.19,"That's the knowledge base."],[12.94,"Every conclusion must follow from these five."]]},{"n":88,"title":"How many worlds now?  Seven symbols","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[8.23,1.43,4.54,4.64],"t":1.28}],"caps":[[0.37,"How many possible worlds does this knowledge base talk about?"],[5.45,"It uses seven symbols:"],[7.48,"two breezes and five pits."],[10.02,"Seven switches."],[11.04,"Watch the count double with each symbol:"],[14.6,"two, four, eight, sixteen, thirty-two, sixty-four, one hundred twenty-eight."],[19.17,"Two to the seventh is 128 possible worlds."]]},{"n":89,"title":"Truth-table enumeration: check all 128 worlds","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.83,7.34,2.34],"t":6.1}],"caps":[[0.61,"Now the computer does what we did by hand:"],[3.96,"it checks every one of the 128 worlds against all five sentences."],[8.43,"I ran this in code."],[10.3,"Only 3 worlds make all five sentences true."],[13.28,"Here they are."],[14.39,"Look at the P one two column, in red:"],[17.75,"it's false in all three."],[19.61,"So the knowledge base entails \"not P one two\"."],[22.96,"No pit in one, two."],[24.83,"Same answer as our hand reasoning, and the same answer as the eight-world version."],[30.04,"More symbols, same idea."]]},{"n":90,"title":"The catch: the table explodes","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.93,12.24,1.24],"t":3.95}],"caps":[[1.05,"Here's the catch."],[2.39,"Truth-table enumeration is sound and complete, but the table doubles with every symbol."],[8.2,"Seven symbols:"],[9.09,"instant."],[9.54,"Twenty symbols:"],[10.43,"about a million rows, still fine."],[13.11,"Fifty symbols:"],[14.0,"about ten to the fifteen rows, which takes years."],[18.02,"A real hospital system with thousands of symbols:"],[21.59,"impossible."],[22.04,"We say the time is order two to the n."],[26.5,"The next two sections are all about doing better."]]},{"n":91,"title":"Equivalence: two sentences, same truth in every world","marks":[],"caps":[[1.11,"Two sentences are equivalent if they're true in exactly the same worlds."],[6.1,"We write it with three horizontal lines."],[9.01,"Compare \"A implies B\" with \"not A or B\"."],[12.76,"Check row by row:"],[14.42,"true, true; true, true; false, false; true, true."],[17.75,"Every row matches."],[19.0,"So they're equivalent, and we can swap one for the other whenever we like."],[24.83,"That's the thought I asked you to hold earlier."],[28.57,"This swap will be very useful when we convert sentences for resolution."]]},{"n":92,"title":"Validity and satisfiability","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,3.99,3.74],"t":2.97},{"k":"box","g":[8.78,1.43,3.99,3.74],"t":4.2},{"k":"box","g":[4.65,1.43,3.99,3.74],"t":9.97},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.33,12.24,0.94],"t":31.37}],"caps":[[0.6,"Three more words."],[1.84,"A sentence is valid if it's true in every world."],[5.95,"\"A or not A\":"],[7.6,"it rains or it doesn't."],[9.65,"Always true."],[10.48,"A sentence is satisfiable if it's true in at least one world."],[15.42,"\"A and B\" is satisfiable:"],[17.47,"just pick the world where both are true."],[20.77,"A sentence is unsatisfiable if it's true in no world at all."],[25.71,"\"A and not A\":"],[27.35,"it rains and it doesn't."],[29.41,"Impossible."],[29.82,"Asking \"is this sentence satisfiable?\" is called the SAT problem."],[33.94,"It comes back in section seven point six."]]},{"n":93,"title":"The bridge to proofs: proof by contradiction","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.76,12.6,2.76],"t":6.52},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":10.47},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":24.33}],"caps":[[0.68,"One last idea, and it's the bridge to the next section."],[5.03,"K B entails alpha exactly when \"K B and not alpha\" is unsatisfiable."],[10.18,"In words:"],[10.97,"to show alpha must be true, assume it's false."],[14.53,"If that assumption contradicts the knowledge base, so that no world can satisfy both,"],[20.07,"then alpha must be true."],[22.05,"Detectives do this all the time."],[24.43,"Suppose the butler did it."],[26.41,"But the butler was in Paris."],[28.78,"Contradiction."],[29.18,"So the butler didn't do it."],[31.56,"This is proof by contradiction, and resolution is built on it."]]},{"n":94,"title":"Recap · 7.4","marks":[],"caps":[[1.0,"Recap of section seven point four."],[3.54,"Atoms are switches."],[4.81,"There are five connectives, and \"or\" is inclusive."],[8.2,"Implies is false only when the premise is true and the conclusion is false."],[14.12,"Truth tables work, but grow as two to the n."],[18.35,"A implies B is equivalent to not A or B."],[22.59,"And K B entails alpha exactly when K B and not alpha is unsatisfiable."],[28.51,"Next:"],[28.93,"proving things without checking every world."]]}]},{"id":"ch7-s5","title":"7.5 Propositional theorem proving","dir":"ch7/s5","slides":[{"n":95,"title":"Propositional Theorem Proving","marks":[],"caps":[[0.76,"Section seven point five:"],[2.36,"theorem proving."],[3.16,"Instead of checking every world, we'll derive conclusions directly,"],[6.76,"one rule at a time, like a chain of reasoning in an argument."]]},{"n":96,"title":"Why prove instead of checking every world?","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.13,12.24,1.09],"t":11.32}],"caps":[[0.67,"Why bother with proofs?"],[2.1,"Because the truth table checked one hundred twenty-eight worlds to show there's no pit"],[7.12,"in one, two."],[8.19,"A proof does it in five short steps."],[11.06,"A proof only touches the sentences that matter for the question,"],[15.0,"and ignores everything else."],[16.43,"In a big knowledge base, that's the difference between a second and a lifetime."]]},{"n":97,"title":"Inference rule 1: Modus Ponens","marks":[],"caps":[[0.66,"An inference rule is a pattern:"],[3.09,"if you have the sentences above the line,"],[6.32,"you're allowed to write the sentence below the line."],[9.96,"The most famous rule is Modus Ponens, Latin for \"the way that affirms\"."],[15.21,"If you have \"alpha implies beta\", and you have alpha, you may write beta."],[20.87,"It's exactly what our hospital agent did at the start."],[24.92,"Difficulty breathing implies urgent evaluation."],[26.94,"Maria has difficulty breathing."],[28.55,"So:"],[28.96,"urgent evaluation."],[29.77,"You've been using Modus Ponens all your life."]]},{"n":98,"title":"Inference rule 2: And-Elimination","marks":[],"caps":[[0.64,"Rule two:"],[1.48,"And-Elimination."],[1.9,"If you have \"alpha and beta\", you may write alpha by itself,"],[6.92,"or beta by itself."],[8.59,"If Maria has a fever and low oxygen,"],[11.94,"then in particular she has low oxygen."],[14.87,"Simple, but we'll need it."]]},{"n":99,"title":"Inference rule 3: Contraposition","marks":[],"caps":[[0.47,"Rule three:"],[1.33,"contraposition."],[1.75,"If alpha implies beta, then not beta implies not alpha."],[6.05,"If rain always makes the grass wet, then dry grass means it did not"],[12.06,"rain."],[12.49,"Notice this is different from the trap we saw."],[16.35,"We're not reversing the arrow; we're flipping it and negating both sides."],[21.5,"That's always safe."]]},{"n":100,"title":"Inference rule 4: De Morgan’s rule","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.53,3.58,6.24,2.59],"t":9.08}],"caps":[[0.63,"Rule four:"],[1.58,"De Morgan's rule."],[3.01,"\"Not, alpha or beta\" becomes \"not alpha, and not beta\"."],[7.76,"Everyday version:"],[8.71,"\"I had neither tea nor coffee\" means \"no tea, and no coffee\"."],[14.41,"The not moves inside and turns the or into an and."]]},{"n":101,"title":"Inference rule 5: Biconditional elimination","marks":[],"caps":[[0.64,"Rule five:"],[1.56,"biconditional elimination."],[2.47,"\"Alpha if and only if beta\" can be split into two arrows:"],[7.96,"alpha implies beta, and beta implies alpha."],[11.16,"That's all the tools we need."],[13.91,"Let's build a real proof."]]},{"n":102,"title":"A proof: no pit in [1,2]","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.38,12.24,0.86],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.72,"Our goal:"],[1.51,"prove there's no pit in square one, two."],[4.66,"We start from two sentences in the knowledge base."],[8.2,"R two:"],[8.99,"one, one is breezy if and only if one, two or two,"],[13.72,"one has a pit."],[15.29,"And R four:"],[16.48,"no breeze in one, one."],[18.44,"Before I go on, think about the plain-English argument."],[21.99,"No breeze in one, one, so no pit next to it."],[26.32,"Our proof will say exactly that, in five careful moves."]]},{"n":103,"title":"A proof: no pit in [1,2]","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.2,12.24,0.86],"t":1.93}],"caps":[[0.64,"Step one."],[1.57,"Apply biconditional elimination to R two."],[4.34,"The if-and-only-if becomes two arrows joined by and:"],[8.03,"B one one implies the or, and the or implies B one one."]]},{"n":104,"title":"A proof: no pit in [1,2]","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.02,12.24,0.86],"t":1.62}],"caps":[[0.65,"Step two."],[1.51,"And-Elimination:"],[1.93,"we only need the second half."],[4.5,"\"P one two or P two one\" implies B one one."],[9.2,"In words:"],[10.05,"a pit next door would cause a breeze."]]},{"n":105,"title":"A proof: no pit in [1,2]","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.84,12.24,0.86],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.48,"Step three."],[1.46,"Contraposition:"],[1.95,"flip and negate."],[3.42,"Not B one one implies, not, P one two or P two one."],[9.8,"In words:"],[10.78,"no breeze means no pit next door."]]},{"n":106,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[],"caps":[[0.59,"Pause and predict."],[1.8,"We have the rule \"not B one one implies, not,"],[5.86,"P one two or P two one\"."],[8.7,"And we have R four:"],[10.72,"not B one one."],[12.34,"Which inference rule gives us the next step?"]]},{"n":107,"title":"A proof: no pit in [1,2]","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.66,12.24,0.86],"t":1.69}],"caps":[[0.55,"The answer is Modus Ponens."],[2.62,"Step four."],[3.44,"Now we have a rule whose if-part is \"not B one one\"."],[8.4,"And R four says exactly that:"],[10.88,"not B one one."],[12.53,"Modus Ponens gives us the then-part:"],[15.01,"not, P one two or P two one."]]},{"n":108,"title":"A proof: no pit in [1,2]","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.48,12.24,0.86],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.91,"Step five."],[1.68,"De Morgan's rule moves the not inside:"],[4.39,"not P one two, and not P two one."],[7.88,"Neither square has a pit."],[9.81,"Five steps, and we never looked at a single truth table row."]]},{"n":109,"title":"Finding a proof is a search problem","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.68,1.43,6.04,1.94],"t":2.45},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.68,12.24,0.74],"t":5.06},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,1.94],"t":8.78},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,3.53,6.04,1.94],"t":14.73},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.53,6.04,1.94],"t":17.33}],"caps":[[0.69,"How did we know which rule to apply?"],[3.67,"In general, finding a proof is a search problem, just like Chapter 3."],[8.5,"The states are the sets of sentences we have so far."],[12.6,"The actions are inference rules."],[14.46,"The start is the knowledge base, and the goal is the sentence we want."],[19.67,"A proof is a path from start to goal."],[23.02,"One nice property:"],[24.13,"adding new facts never cancels a proof."],[26.74,"Logic is monotonic:"],[27.85,"once something is proved, it stays proved."]]},{"n":110,"title":"Resolution, the everyday version","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.56,12.6,2.56],"t":7.93}],"caps":[[0.65,"Now a single rule that turns out to be enough for everything:"],[5.08,"resolution."],[5.45,"Let's start with the everyday version."],[7.66,"My keys are in my bag or in my car."],[11.35,"They're not in my car."],[13.19,"So they're in my bag."],[15.04,"You did that without thinking."],[16.88,"One option was eliminated, so the other one must hold."]]},{"n":111,"title":"Resolution: cancel a matching pair","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[3.7,4.76,9.6,4.76],"t":3.04},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.93,12.24,1.34],"t":13.6}],"caps":[[0.71,"Here's the same thing in symbols."],[3.14,"\"Bag or Car\", and \"not Car\"."],[5.58,"The two contain a matching pair:"],[8.02,"Car, and not Car."],[9.64,"They cancel each other out."],[11.67,"What's left is Bag."],[13.29,"In general:"],[14.1,"take two clauses that contain a symbol and its opposite."],[18.16,"Remove the pair, and join whatever is left with \"or\"."],[22.22,"From \"A or B\" and \"not B or C\", you get \"A or C\"."]]},{"n":112,"title":"Resolution finds the pit in [3,1]","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.19,12.24,0.92],"t":23.07}],"caps":[[0.78,"Now the cave."],[1.98,"Remember how we found the pit by hand?"],[5.18,"Resolution does it mechanically."],[6.78,"Clause one:"],[7.58,"the breeze in two, one means a pit in one, one, or two,"],[12.78,"two, or three, one."],[14.38,"Clause two:"],[15.18,"from square one, two, we know there's no pit in two, two."],[19.97,"P two two and not P two two cancel."],[23.57,"What's left:"],[24.37,"P one one or P three one."]]},{"n":113,"title":"Resolution finds the pit in [3,1]","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.95,12.24,0.92],"t":1.13}],"caps":[[0.84,"Clause four:"],[1.62,"no pit in the start square, not P one one."],[5.51,"Resolve it with clause three."],[7.46,"P one one and not P one one cancel."],[10.97,"What's left:"],[11.75,"P three one."],[12.92,"The pit is in three, one."],[15.25,"That's exactly the conclusion we reached by hand in the wumpus walkthrough,"],[19.93,"now produced by one mechanical rule."]]},{"n":114,"title":"The words resolution uses","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,3.99,3.14],"t":2.9},{"k":"box","g":[4.65,1.43,3.99,3.14],"t":9.6},{"k":"box","g":[8.78,1.43,3.99,3.14],"t":16.29},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.73,12.24,1.44],"t":24.16}],"caps":[[0.64,"A few words resolution uses."],[2.61,"A literal is a symbol or its negation,"],[5.76,"like P three one or not P two two."],[9.3,"A clause is literals joined by \"or\"."],[12.06,"And the empty clause, drawn as a little box,"],[15.6,"is a clause with nothing left in it."],[18.75,"An empty \"or\" can never be true, so it stands for a contradiction."],[23.87,"One catch:"],[24.66,"resolution only works on clauses."],[26.62,"So first, we rewrite every sentence into clauses."],[29.77,"That form is called C N F."]]},{"n":115,"title":"Conjunctive normal form (CNF): an AND of ORs","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":10.74},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":18.86}],"caps":[[0.69,"Conjunctive normal form, or C N F, is an \"and\" of \"or\"s:"],[5.56,"a list of clauses, all joined by \"and\"."],[8.81,"Think of it as a checklist."],[11.24,"Every line must be satisfied, and each line is satisfied if at least one"],[16.93,"of its items is true."],[18.96,"Good news:"],[19.77,"every sentence can be rewritten in C N F, using four mechanical steps."],[25.04,"Let's convert R two, one step per slide."]]},{"n":116,"title":"Converting R₂ to CNF","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.38,12.24,0.92],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[1.05,"Start with R two:"],[2.51,"B one one if and only if, P one two or P two one."]]},{"n":117,"title":"Converting R₂ to CNF","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.26,12.24,0.92],"t":1.87}],"caps":[[0.82,"Step one:"],[1.97,"remove the if-and-only-if."],[3.69,"It becomes two arrows, joined by and."]]},{"n":118,"title":"Converting R₂ to CNF","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.14,12.24,0.92],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.74,"Step two:"],[1.55,"remove each arrow, using the equivalence from section seven point four:"],[6.02,"alpha implies beta becomes not alpha or beta."],[9.27,"The first arrow becomes \"not B one one, or P one two,"],[14.15,"or P two one\"."],[15.77,"The second becomes \"not, P one two or P two one,"],[20.24,"or B one one\"."]]},{"n":119,"title":"Converting R₂ to CNF","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.02,12.24,0.92],"t":1.23}],"caps":[[0.56,"Step three:"],[1.33,"move the not inward, until it only sits on single symbols."],[5.59,"De Morgan turns \"not, P one two or P two one\" into \"not P"],[11.0,"one two, and not P two one\"."]]},{"n":120,"title":"Converting R₂ to CNF","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.9,12.24,0.92],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.68,"Step four:"],[1.49,"distribute \"or\" over \"and\", like multiplying out brackets in algebra."],[5.54,"\"Not P one two and not P two one,"],[9.2,"or B one one\" becomes two clauses:"],[12.04,"\"not P one two or B one one\","],[15.28,"and \"not P two one or B one one\"."],[18.94,"The result:"],[19.75,"three clauses."],[20.56,"R two is now in C N F, ready for resolution."]]},{"n":121,"title":"Resolution proves things by contradiction","marks":[],"caps":[[1.18,"Resolution proves things by contradiction, the butler idea from section seven point four."],[6.88,"Step one:"],[7.76,"write the knowledge base as clauses."],[10.39,"Step two:"],[11.27,"add the opposite of what you want to prove."],[15.21,"Step three:"],[16.09,"resolve pairs of clauses, again and again."],[19.16,"Step four:"],[20.04,"if you ever produce the empty clause, you've found a contradiction,"],[24.86,"so the goal must be true."],[27.49,"Let's prove, once more, that there's no pit in one, two."]]},{"n":122,"title":"A resolution proof that [1,2] has no pit","marks":[],"caps":[[0.66,"Here's our knowledge base as clauses:"],[2.81,"the three clauses from R two, and R four, not B one one."],[7.47,"The goal is \"not P one two\"."]]},{"n":123,"title":"A resolution proof that [1,2] has no pit","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[10.53,1.93,2.24,0.89],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.86,"Step two:"],[1.54,"add the opposite of the goal."],[3.59,"The goal is \"not P one two\", so we add P one two."],[8.03,"We're assuming, for the sake of argument, that there is a pit in one,"],[12.81,"two."]]},{"n":124,"title":"A resolution proof that [1,2] has no pit","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[3.03,1.93,2.44,0.89],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[10.53,1.93,2.24,0.89],"t":1.5},{"k":"box","g":[5.53,3.53,2.44,0.89],"t":2.4}],"caps":[[0.98,"Now resolve."],[1.71,"Our assumption, P one two, and clause two,"],[4.65,"\"not P one two or B one one\", contain a matching pair."],[9.05,"P one two and not P one two cancel."],[12.35,"What's left:"],[13.08,"B one one."],[14.18,"In words:"],[14.92,"if there were a pit in one, two,"],[17.85,"there would be a breeze in one, one."]]},{"n":125,"title":"A resolution proof that [1,2] has no pit","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[8.03,1.93,2.44,0.89],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[10.53,1.93,2.24,0.89],"t":1.5},{"k":"box","g":[5.53,3.53,2.44,0.89],"t":2.4}],"caps":[[0.96,"Now resolve B one one with clause four, not B one one."],[5.94,"They cancel, and nothing is left."],[8.43,"The empty clause!"],[9.68,"A contradiction:"],[10.51,"the assumption predicts a breeze, but the agent felt none."]]},{"n":126,"title":"A resolution proof that [1,2] has no pit","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[10.53,1.93,2.24,0.89],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[5.53,3.53,2.44,0.89],"t":1.5}],"caps":[[0.71,"So the assumption was impossible."],[2.74,"There is no pit in one, two."],[5.57,"Proved by resolution, in two resolution steps."],[8.41,"Resolution is sound, and it's also complete for this kind of question:"],[13.27,"if K B entails alpha, resolution will find the empty clause."],[17.72,"One single rule is enough for all of propositional logic."]]},{"n":127,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[],"caps":[[0.68,"Pause and predict."],[1.98,"Resolve \"A or B\" with \"not A or B\"."],[5.9,"Which pair cancels, and what's left?"]]},{"n":128,"title":"Answer: B","marks":[],"caps":[[0.43,"The answer is B."],[2.02,"A and not A cancel, leaving \"B or B\", which is just B."],[7.17,"It makes sense:"],[8.36,"if A is true, the second clause forces B."],[11.93,"If A is false, the first clause forces B."],[15.5,"Either way, B."]]},{"n":129,"title":"A special, easy kind of clause: definite clauses","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":9.51},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":28.33}],"caps":[[1.01,"Most real knowledge comes in a friendly shape."],[4.29,"Look at this clause:"],[5.92,"not A, or not B, or L."],[8.79,"It has exactly one positive literal, L."],[11.65,"A clause like that is called a definite clause."],[15.33,"And here's the nice part:"],[17.38,"every definite clause can be read as an if-then rule."],[21.47,"\"Not A or not B or L\" is the same as \"A and B"],[27.2,"implies L\"."],[28.02,"That's how people naturally write knowledge, as rules."],[31.3,"And reasoning with these rules is fast:"],[34.16,"linear time."],[34.98,"Clauses with at most one positive literal are called Horn clauses."],[39.48,"Let's reason with them, in two directions."]]},{"n":130,"title":"Forward chaining: start from the facts","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.26,1.35,0.9,0.82],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[1.03,"Forward chaining starts from what we know, and fires every rule it can,"],[6.51,"until the question is answered."],[8.62,"Here's the textbook's knowledge base."],[10.72,"Five rules:"],[11.57,"P implies Q."],[12.83,"L and M imply P."],[14.94,"B and L imply M."],[17.05,"A and P imply L."],[19.15,"A and B imply L."],[21.26,"And two facts:"],[22.53,"A and B."],[23.79,"Our question:"],[24.63,"is Q true?"],[25.9,"We start with the facts, A and B, shown dark in the graph."]]},{"n":131,"title":"Forward chaining: start from the facts","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.26,1.35,0.9,0.82],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[5.53,4.28,7.24,0.64],"t":1.5}],"caps":[[0.74,"Look for a rule whose premises are all known."],[4.37,"\"A and B implies L\":"],[6.38,"A is known, B is known."],[8.8,"It fires!"],[9.6,"L is now known."]]},{"n":132,"title":"Forward chaining: start from the facts","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.26,1.35,0.9,0.82],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[5.53,3.08,7.24,0.64],"t":1.5}],"caps":[[0.92,"With L known, \"B and L implies M\" has both premises."],[5.94,"It fires:"],[6.86,"M is known."]]},{"n":133,"title":"Forward chaining: start from the facts","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.26,1.35,0.9,0.82],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[5.53,2.48,7.24,0.64],"t":1.5}],"caps":[[0.78,"Now \"L and M implies P\" fires:"],[3.82,"P is known."]]},{"n":134,"title":"Forward chaining: start from the facts","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[5.53,1.88,7.24,0.64],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.63,"And \"P implies Q\" fires:"],[2.83,"Q is known."],[4.15,"That's our query, so we stop."],[6.79,"Q is proved."],[8.1,"The order, A, B, L, M, P, Q,"],[11.62,"is exactly what the algorithm produces when I run it."]]},{"n":135,"title":"Forward chaining in one slide","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,3.99,3.54],"t":4.26},{"k":"box","g":[4.65,1.43,3.99,3.54],"t":9.49},{"k":"box","g":[8.78,1.43,3.99,3.54],"t":13.79},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.13,12.24,1.09],"t":19.96}],"caps":[[0.74,"Forward chaining in one slide."],[2.75,"It repeats one move:"],[4.36,"find a rule whose premises are all known, and add its conclusion."],[9.19,"It's sound, complete for definite clauses, and runs in linear time."],[13.62,"It's data-driven:"],[14.43,"it notices a fact and draws every consequence, even ones nobody asked about."],[19.66,"A hospital alert system works like this:"],[22.48,"a lab result arrives, and every alert that follows fires at once."]]},{"n":136,"title":"Backward chaining: start from the question","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[5.53,1.93,7.24,1.09],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.84,"Backward chaining works the other way round."],[3.72,"It starts from the question and works backward to the facts."],[8.23,"Goal:"],[8.64,"Q."],[9.05,"Which rule has Q as its conclusion?"],[11.92,"\"P implies Q\"."],[13.15,"So to prove Q, we need to prove P."],[16.84,"P becomes our new goal."]]},{"n":137,"title":"Backward chaining: start from the question","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.26,1.35,0.9,0.82],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[5.88,3.08,6.89,1.09],"t":7.35}],"caps":[[0.77,"New goal:"],[1.66,"P."],[2.11,"The rule \"L and M implies P\" concludes P."],[6.11,"So now we need two things:"],[8.78,"L, and M."],[10.12,"Let's take L first."]]},{"n":138,"title":"Backward chaining: start from the question","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.26,1.35,0.9,0.82],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[2.26,2.55,0.9,0.82],"t":1.5},{"k":"box","g":[6.23,4.23,6.54,1.09],"t":2.4}],"caps":[[0.96,"Goal:"],[1.35,"L."],[1.73,"The first rule that concludes L is \"A and P implies L\"."],[6.31,"But that needs P, and P is what we're already trying to prove."],[11.27,"That's going in a circle."],[13.18,"A careful backward chainer notices the loop and skips this rule."]]},{"n":139,"title":"Backward chaining: start from the question","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.26,1.35,0.9,0.82],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[6.23,4.23,6.54,1.09],"t":1.5}],"caps":[[1.03,"Try the other rule for L:"],[3.37,"\"A and B implies L\"."],[5.33,"A is a fact."],[6.9,"B is a fact."],[8.46,"So L is proved!"]]},{"n":140,"title":"Backward chaining: start from the question","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.26,1.35,0.9,0.82],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[6.23,4.23,6.54,1.09],"t":7.24}],"caps":[[0.7,"Back to P's other need:"],[2.77,"M."],[3.19,"The rule \"B and L implies M\"."],[6.09,"B is a fact, and we just proved L."],[9.83,"So M is proved."]]},{"n":141,"title":"Backward chaining: start from the question","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.23,4.23,6.54,1.09],"t":6.64}],"caps":[[0.79,"With L and M proved, P is proved."],[3.96,"And with P proved, Q is proved."],[6.74,"Done!"],[7.14,"Notice that backward chaining only touched rules relevant to Q."],[11.11,"In a big knowledge base, that's a huge saving."]]},{"n":142,"title":"Forward or backward?","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,3.44],"t":3.54},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,3.44],"t":14.18},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.03,12.24,1.14],"t":24.83}],"caps":[[0.66,"So which one should an agent use?"],[3.64,"Forward chaining starts from the facts."],[6.19,"It's data-driven, and it may derive many facts nobody asked about."],[10.88,"Like a nurse reacting to every new reading."],[14.28,"Backward chaining starts from the question."],[16.84,"It's goal-driven, and only touches what's relevant."],[19.82,"Like a doctor who's asked one specific question:"],[23.23,"does Maria need monitoring?"],[24.93,"Both are sound and complete for definite clauses."],[28.34,"Use forward chaining for monitoring, and backward chaining for answering questions."]]},{"n":143,"title":"Recap · 7.5","marks":[],"caps":[[0.73,"Recap of section seven point five."],[3.16,"Inference rules like Modus Ponens let us build proofs,"],[6.82,"which are searches from the knowledge base to the goal."],[10.87,"Resolution cancels a matching pair; it's one rule, sound and complete."],[15.34,"It needs C N F, an and of ors."],[18.99,"And for rule-shaped knowledge, forward chaining reasons from facts,"],[22.64,"while backward chaining reasons from the question."],[25.48,"Next:"],[25.89,"faster ways to check models."]]}]},{"id":"ch7-s6","title":"7.6 Effective model checking","dir":"ch7/s6","slides":[{"n":144,"title":"Effective Propositional Model Checking","marks":[],"caps":[[0.66,"Section seven point six:"],[2.36,"effective model checking."],[3.64,"The truth table checks every world."],[6.2,"Now we learn to check worlds smartly, skipping almost all of them."]]},{"n":145,"title":"The SAT question","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.76,12.6,2.76],"t":2.98},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":12.34},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":22.58}],"caps":[[0.85,"Here's the SAT question:"],[2.63,"is there any world that makes every clause true?"],[6.64,"Why do we care?"],[8.43,"Because of the butler idea:"],[10.65,"K B entails alpha exactly when K B and not alpha has no satisfying"],[16.89,"world."],[17.34,"So a fast SAT checker is a fast entailment checker."],[21.79,"And SAT shows up everywhere:"],[24.02,"scheduling nurses, checking computer chips, planning, even Sudoku."],[27.58,"Two algorithms:"],[28.48,"DPLL, which is systematic, and WalkSAT, which is a local search."]]},{"n":146,"title":"DPLL: three ideas that beat the truth table","marks":[],"caps":[[0.54,"DPLL is named after its four inventors:"],[3.24,"Davis, Putnam, Logemann, and Loveland."],[5.18,"It's a smart truth-table search built on three ideas."],[8.65,"Idea one:"],[9.43,"stop early."],[10.2,"A clause is true as soon as one of its literals is true."],[15.23,"And the whole sentence is false as soon as one clause is false."],[20.25,"So we don't need to fill in every symbol before we know the answer."]]},{"n":147,"title":"DPLL: three ideas that beat the truth table","marks":[],"caps":[[0.62,"Idea two:"],[1.4,"pure symbols."],[2.18,"If a symbol appears with only one sign, always positive or always negated,"],[7.26,"we can safely set it to make those literals true."],[11.17,"It can't hurt any clause."],[13.12,"If D only ever appears as plain D, set D to true."]]},{"n":148,"title":"DPLL: three ideas that beat the truth table","marks":[],"caps":[[0.64,"Idea three:"],[1.47,"unit clauses."],[2.3,"If a clause has only one literal left that isn't decided,"],[6.88,"that literal is forced."],[8.54,"If the clause is \"A or C\", and A is false,"],[13.11,"then C must be true."],[15.19,"No guessing."],[16.02,"When none of these apply, DPLL guesses a value,"],[19.77,"and backtracks if the guess fails, just like backtracking search in Chapter 5."]]},{"n":149,"title":"DPLL in action","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[7.13,3.53,5.64,1.54],"t":13.42}],"caps":[[0.64,"Let's trace DPLL on four clauses:"],[3.4,"A or B."],[4.78,"Not A or C."],[6.62,"Not B or not C."],[8.92,"Not C or D."],[10.76,"Nothing is assigned yet."],[12.6,"Find the move before I make it."]]},{"n":150,"title":"DPLL in action","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[9.83,1.93,2.99,1.24],"t":14.57}],"caps":[[0.85,"First, any pure symbols?"],[2.58,"A appears both plain and negated."],[5.17,"So do B and C."],[7.33,"But D appears only as plain D."],[10.35,"It's pure, so set D true."],[12.94,"Clause four is now satisfied."]]},{"n":151,"title":"DPLL in action","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.93,2.99,1.24],"t":18.2}],"caps":[[0.73,"Now, with clause four gone, A, B, and C each appear both ways,"],[6.17,"so there are no pure symbols."],[8.68,"And no clause has just one literal left."],[12.02,"So DPLL has to guess."],[14.11,"Try A equals true."],[15.79,"Clause one, A or B, is satisfied."]]},{"n":152,"title":"DPLL in action","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[3.63,1.93,2.99,1.24],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.55,"With A true, clause two, not A or C, has only C left."],[6.11,"That's a unit clause."],[7.82,"C is forced:"],[9.1,"C equals true."]]},{"n":153,"title":"DPLL in action","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.93,2.99,1.24],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.9,"With C true, clause three, not B or not C,"],[5.08,"has only not B left."],[7.18,"Another unit clause."],[8.43,"B is forced to false."]]},{"n":154,"title":"DPLL in action","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,6.31,12.6,6.31],"t":9.36}],"caps":[[0.76,"Every clause is now satisfied."],[3.18,"The answer:"],[4.15,"satisfiable, with A true, B false, C true, D true."],[8.98,"One guess, and three forced moves."],[11.88,"The truth table would have needed up to sixteen rows."],[16.71,"My code produced the same model."]]},{"n":155,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[],"caps":[[0.7,"Pause and predict."],[2.17,"DPLL is checking \"K B and not alpha\", and it reports:"],[7.58,"unsatisfiable."],[8.07,"What does that tell us about alpha?"]]},{"n":156,"title":"Answer: KB ⊨ α","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,3.34],"t":3.73},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,3.34],"t":14.37}],"caps":[[0.42,"The answer is A:"],[2.12,"K B entails alpha."],[3.83,"Unsatisfiable means no world makes K B true and alpha false,"],[8.51,"so alpha is true in every world where K B is true."],[13.62,"And because DPLL is complete, it never gives up."],[17.45,"It either finds a model or proves there is none."],[21.7,"So \"unsatisfiable\" is a real proof."]]},{"n":157,"title":"WalkSAT: a different approach — local search","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,4.74],"t":3.42},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,4.74],"t":5.36}],"caps":[[0.66,"WalkSAT takes a completely different approach:"],[2.88,"local search, like min-conflicts in Chapter 5."],[5.46,"Start with a random setting of every switch."],[8.42,"While some clause is false, pick one false clause and flip one of its"],[13.59,"switches."],[13.96,"Usually, flip the switch that makes the most clauses true."],[17.65,"Sometimes, with a small probability, flip a random one instead,"],[21.35,"so we don't get stuck."],[23.2,"It doesn't build a search tree at all."],[26.15,"It starts with a complete but wrong answer and repairs it."]]},{"n":158,"title":"WalkSAT in action","marks":[],"caps":[[0.99,"Let's run WalkSAT on the same four clauses."],[4.63,"A random start:"],[5.99,"A, B, C true, D false."],[8.72,"Two clauses are false:"],[10.54,"\"not B or not C\", and \"not C or D\"."]]},{"n":159,"title":"WalkSAT in action","marks":[],"caps":[[0.67,"Pick a false clause:"],[2.31,"\"not B or not C\"."],[4.35,"Its switches are B and C."],[6.81,"Flipping B leaves one false clause; flipping C also leaves one."],[11.31,"It's a tie, so take B."],[13.76,"Now B is false, and only one clause is still false."]]},{"n":160,"title":"WalkSAT in action","marks":[],"caps":[[0.81,"The remaining false clause is \"not C or D\"."],[4.74,"Flipping C would break another clause; flipping D breaks nothing."],[9.11,"So flip D."],[10.42,"Every clause is true!"],[12.16,"Two flips, and the same model DPLL found."],[15.66,"I checked this trace in code."]]},{"n":161,"title":"WalkSAT: fast, but it cannot say “no”","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,3.34],"t":3.23},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,3.34],"t":11.72},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.93,12.24,1.24],"t":20.62}],"caps":[[0.9,"What can WalkSAT promise?"],[2.52,"If it finds a model, the sentence is definitely satisfiable,"],[6.56,"and on big problems it's often much faster than DPLL."],[10.61,"But if it gives up, we learn nothing for sure."],[14.65,"Maybe there's no model, or maybe it just didn't find one."],[19.1,"It's incomplete."],[19.91,"That's why WalkSAT is great for finding solutions, but it can't prove entailment,"],[25.17,"because entailment needs a proof of \"unsatisfiable\"."]]},{"n":162,"title":"Where the hard problems are","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[8.23,1.33,4.54,1.59],"t":15.53},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,4.68,4.54,1.69],"t":15.91},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,2.93,4.54,1.74],"t":27.4}],"caps":[[0.7,"One last surprise."],[1.85,"Which SAT problems are hard?"],[3.76,"I generated random problems with twenty symbols and three literals per clause,"],[8.36,"and counted how many were satisfiable as the number of clauses grows."],[12.95,"This chart is my own measurement."],[15.25,"With few clauses per symbol, almost every problem is satisfiable, and easy."],[19.84,"With many clauses, almost none are, and that's easy to prove too."],[24.44,"The hard problems sit in the middle, where about half are satisfiable."],[29.03,"With twenty symbols, the drop is near four point four,"],[32.86,"slightly to the right of the textbook's four point two six,"],[37.07,"because the textbook's number is for very large problems."],[40.51,"That narrow band is where SAT solvers sweat."]]},{"n":163,"title":"Recap · 7.6","marks":[],"caps":[[0.9,"Recap of section seven point six."],[3.67,"SAT asks whether any world satisfies every clause,"],[7.38,"and entailment is just \"K B and not alpha is unsatisfiable\"."],[12.47,"DPLL is a smart, complete search using early stopping, pure symbols, and unit clauses."],[18.95,"WalkSAT is fast local search, but incomplete."],[22.19,"And the hard problems cluster where about half are satisfiable."],[26.82,"Next:"],[27.28,"putting all of this inside an agent that moves through time."]]}]},{"id":"ch7-s7","title":"7.7 Agents based on propositional logic","dir":"ch7/s7","slides":[{"n":164,"title":"Agents Based on Propositional Logic","marks":[],"caps":[[0.76,"Section seven point seven:"],[2.58,"agents based on propositional logic."],[4.85,"Everything so far described a world that sits still."],[8.94,"Real agents move, and the world changes."]]},{"n":165,"title":"Facts that change: fluents","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,2.74],"t":1.36},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,2.74],"t":11.0},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.33,12.24,1.74],"t":24.11}],"caps":[[0.69,"Here's the problem."],[1.84,"\"The agent is in square one, one\" is true now,"],[5.7,"and false after it moves."],[7.63,"One symbol can't be both true and false."],[10.71,"The fix:"],[11.48,"stamp a time on it."],[13.41,"L, superscript zero, subscript one one, means the agent is in one,"],[18.04,"one at time zero."],[19.58,"L superscript one, subscript two one, means it's in two, one at time one."],[24.98,"A fact that can change is called a fluent."],[28.45,"Facts that never change, like where the pits are, don't need a time stamp."]]},{"n":166,"title":"A short timeline","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.93,12.24,1.24],"t":11.75}],"caps":[[1.06,"Here's a short timeline."],[2.41,"At time zero, the agent is in one, one, and it has its arrow."],[7.13,"It takes the action Forward."],[8.82,"At time one, it's in two, one, and it still has its arrow."],[13.2,"But wait."],[13.88,"How does the agent know it still has the arrow at time one?"],[18.26,"Nobody told it that moving forward doesn't use up the arrow."],[21.97,"That gap is a famous problem."]]},{"n":167,"title":"The frame problem: saying what does NOT change","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,4.74],"t":6.06},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,4.74],"t":24.0}],"caps":[[0.7,"This is called the frame problem:"],[3.04,"how do you say what does not change?"],[6.16,"Imagine writing a diary like this."],[8.5,"\"I brushed my teeth."],[10.06,"My car is still in the driveway."],[12.79,"My shoes are still in the closet."],[15.52,"The moon is still in the sky.\" Writing what changes is easy."],[20.2,"Listing everything that stays the same never ends."],[23.32,"For the agent:"],[24.49,"turning left doesn't move the gold."],[26.83,"Moving forward doesn't use the arrow."],[29.17,"Logic knows none of this unless we write it down."]]},{"n":168,"title":"The fix: one successor-state axiom per fluent","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.76,12.6,2.76],"t":19.12},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":23.5},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":31.08}],"caps":[[1.27,"The fix is clever."],[2.87,"Instead of listing what every action doesn't change,"],[6.06,"we write one sentence per fluent, saying exactly when it's true at the next"],[11.64,"step."],[12.04,"It's called a successor-state axiom."],[14.03,"For the arrow:"],[15.23,"HaveArrow at time t plus one, if and only if,"],[19.22,"HaveArrow at time t and not Shoot at time t."],[23.2,"In words:"],[24.0,"the agent has its arrow next step exactly when it has it now and"],[29.58,"doesn't shoot now."],[30.78,"One sentence covers every action at once."],[33.57,"Forward, turn left, grab:"],[35.16,"none of them is Shoot, so the arrow stays."],[38.75,"Frame problem solved, for this fluent."]]},{"n":169,"title":"A hybrid agent for the wumpus world","marks":[],"caps":[[0.68,"Here's how a practical wumpus agent puts it all together."],[4.13,"It's called a hybrid agent, because it combines logic with search."],[7.93,"Every turn, it TELLs the knowledge base its percepts,"],[11.03,"then goes down a list of priorities."],[13.45,"Priority one:"],[14.14,"if it sees a glitter, grab the gold,"],[16.89,"plan a safe route home using A star search, and climb out."]]},{"n":170,"title":"A hybrid agent for the wumpus world","marks":[],"caps":[[0.88,"Priority two:"],[1.56,"otherwise, ASK the knowledge base which squares are provably safe,"],[4.96,"and plan a route to the nearest one it hasn't visited."],[8.69,"This is exactly the reasoning we did by hand."]]},{"n":171,"title":"A hybrid agent for the wumpus world","marks":[],"caps":[[0.67,"Priority three:"],[1.36,"if it knows where the wumpus is, and still has its arrow, shoot it."]]},{"n":172,"title":"A hybrid agent for the wumpus world","marks":[],"caps":[[0.74,"Priority four:"],[1.63,"if nothing is provably safe, go to a square that isn't provably unsafe."],[7.41,"That's a calculated risk."]]},{"n":173,"title":"A hybrid agent for the wumpus world","marks":[],"caps":[[0.68,"Priority five:"],[1.56,"if all else fails, go home and climb out."],[5.55,"Logic decides what's safe."],[7.32,"Search plans how to get there."]]},{"n":174,"title":"Planning as SAT: SATPlan","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.76,12.6,2.76],"t":13.73},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.93,3.94,2.54],"t":19.86},{"k":"box","g":[4.68,2.93,3.94,2.54],"t":19.86},{"k":"box","g":[8.83,2.93,3.94,2.54],"t":20.68}],"caps":[[0.76,"One more way logic helps an agent:"],[3.62,"planning."],[4.03,"The idea is called SATPlan."],[6.07,"Write the start state, the successor-state axioms, and the goal at time T."],[11.38,"Then ask a SAT solver:"],[13.42,"is there a world where the goal is true at time T?"],[18.33,"Try T equals one."],[19.96,"Unsatisfiable:"],[20.37,"the plan can't be that short."],[22.82,"T equals two, also unsatisfiable."],[24.86,"T equals three:"],[26.09,"satisfiable!"],[26.49,"And the satisfying world tells us the plan,"],[29.76,"because it says which action symbols are true at each time step."],[34.66,"This illustration is schematic; the real number of steps depends on the problem."]]},{"n":175,"title":"Recap · 7.7","marks":[],"caps":[[0.69,"Recap of section seven point seven."],[2.97,"Fluents are facts that change, stamped with a time."],[6.39,"The frame problem is saying what doesn't change, and successor-state axioms solve it."],[11.33,"A hybrid agent uses logic to decide what's safe and search to plan the"],[16.64,"route."],[17.02,"And SATPlan finds a plan by finding a model."],[20.44,"Now let's bring everything together at Northgate Hospital."]]}]},{"id":"ch7-s8","title":"Northgate Hospital and wrap-up","dir":"ch7/s8","slides":[{"n":176,"title":"Case Study: Northgate Hospital","marks":[],"caps":[[0.71,"Back to Northgate Hospital."],[2.68,"In Chapter 5, Maria's room, bed, and nurse were variables to assign."],[8.6,"Now the hospital's AI is a logical agent."],[12.54,"It keeps a knowledge base of facts and rules about Maria,"],[17.96,"and it has to work out what follows, and, just as importantly,"],[23.87,"what does not."]]},{"n":177,"title":"Scenario 1 · Identifying an urgent patient","marks":[],"caps":[[0.61,"Scenario one."],[1.37,"Maria arrives with a high fever and difficulty breathing."],[4.78,"Here's the knowledge base."],[6.3,"Two facts:"],[7.06,"high fever, and difficulty breathing."],[8.96,"Two rules:"],[9.71,"rule three says difficulty breathing implies urgent evaluation."],[12.75,"Rule four says urgent evaluation implies being seen by an emergency physician."],[17.3,"Which method fits?"],[18.44,"These are definite clauses, so forward chaining."],[21.1,"Predict the first rule to fire before the next slide."]]},{"n":178,"title":"Scenario 1 · Identifying an urgent patient","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.88,7.54,0.74],"t":0.91}],"caps":[[1.01,"Rule three's premise, difficulty breathing, is a known fact."],[5.44,"So rule three fires:"],[7.41,"Maria requires an urgent evaluation."]]},{"n":179,"title":"Scenario 1 · Identifying an urgent patient","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.58,7.54,0.74],"t":2.24}],"caps":[[0.71,"That new fact makes rule four's premise true."],[3.98,"It fires:"],[4.8,"Maria should be seen by an emergency physician."],[8.07,"No other rule can fire, so forward chaining stops."]]},{"n":180,"title":"Scenario 1 · Identifying an urgent patient","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[8.33,1.83,4.44,4.54],"t":4.67}],"caps":[[0.66,"Now the equally important question:"],[2.71,"what can't the agent conclude?"],[4.77,"Which emergency physician."],[6.0,"Which room."],[6.82,"Which treatment."],[7.64,"The knowledge base has no facts or rules about any of them."],[12.56,"And notice:"],[13.38,"the high fever was never used, because no rule mentions it."],[17.9,"A logical agent derives exactly what its facts and rules support."],[22.41,"No more."]]},{"n":181,"title":"Scenario 10 · A rule with two conditions","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,3.14],"t":7.35},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,3.08,6.04,1.49],"t":14.98},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.73,12.24,1.44],"t":22.61}],"caps":[[0.91,"Scenario ten."],[2.0,"Northgate has a stricter rule for continuous respiratory monitoring."],[6.91,"Three symbols."],[8.0,"D:"],[8.54,"Maria has difficulty breathing."],[10.72,"L:"],[11.27,"Maria has low oxygen saturation."],[13.99,"M:"],[14.54,"Maria needs continuous monitoring."],[16.72,"The rule:"],[17.81,"D and L implies M."],[20.53,"Monitoring needs both conditions."],[22.71,"Question:"],[23.26,"if the knowledge base knows only D, plus the rule, does it entail M?"],[30.89,"Three symbols means eight worlds."],[33.62,"Let's check them."]]},{"n":182,"title":"KB₁ = { D,  (D ∧ L) ⇒ M }: which worlds fit?","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,6.36,12.6,6.36],"t":7.19}],"caps":[[0.64,"Here are all eight worlds."],[2.6,"Keep only those where D is true and the rule is kept."],[7.29,"3 worlds fit."],[8.46,"Look at the red one:"],[10.41,"D true, L false, M false."],[12.76,"The knowledge base is true there, because the rule's if-part, D and L,"],[17.84,"is false, so the rule isn't broken."],[20.57,"But M is false."],[22.14,"That's a counterexample."],[23.31,"So K B one does not entail M."],[26.44,"Knowing D alone doesn't establish D and L."]]},{"n":183,"title":"KB₂ = { D,  L,  (D ∧ L) ⇒ M }: add the oxygen reading","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[9.793,3.355,2.977,1.865],"t":6.61},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,6.36,12.6,6.36],"t":8.25}],"caps":[[0.98,"Now a nurse records low oxygen saturation."],[3.84,"TELL the knowledge base:"],[5.48,"L."],[5.89,"Check the eight worlds again."],[7.94,"Now only 1 world fits, the one where D, L,"],[12.03,"and M are all true."],[14.08,"M is true in every fitting world, so K B two entails M."],[19.4,"Maria needs continuous monitoring."],[21.04,"I checked both cases by enumerating all eight worlds in code."]]},{"n":184,"title":"Scenario 10 · The lesson","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.93,12.24,1.24],"t":2.15}],"caps":[[0.96,"The lesson:"],[1.82,"a conjunction needs every one of its parts."],[5.25,"With only D, monitoring is unknown."],[7.82,"Add L, and it's proved."],[9.97,"Adding a fact never cancels a conclusion, but it can turn \"unknown\" into \"proved\"."]]},{"n":185,"title":"Scenario 11 · When the system cannot conclude","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.36,12.6,2.36],"t":6.65},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,4.88,6.04,1.44],"t":33.48}],"caps":[[0.88,"Scenario eleven:"],[1.71,"when the system cannot conclude."],[3.81,"The knowledge base says:"],[5.49,"if Maria has pneumonia, P, then she receives a respiratory evaluation, R."],[10.52,"And it knows R:"],[12.2,"she received one."],[13.45,"Can it conclude P?"],[15.13,"You've seen this trap."],[16.81,"Two worlds fit."],[18.07,"In one, she has pneumonia."],[20.16,"In the other, highlighted, she doesn't; she was evaluated for another reason."],[25.2,"So the knowledge base does not entail P."],[28.55,"That's affirming the consequent."],[30.23,"In a hospital it's a real error:"],[33.16,"an evaluation can follow from asthma, a fever, or a routine check."],[38.19,"Treating Maria for pneumonia because she was evaluated would be wrong."]]},{"n":186,"title":"Northgate: derived — and cannot be derived","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,4.34],"t":10.86},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,4.34],"t":14.66},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.88,12.24,0.89],"t":26.04}],"caps":[[1.0,"Let's collect what Northgate's logical agent can and can't conclude."],[5.75,"Derived:"],[6.22,"urgent evaluation, and the emergency-physician requirement, from the symptoms."],[10.49,"Continuous monitoring, once both conditions are known."],[13.81,"Cannot be derived:"],[15.23,"monitoring from difficulty breathing alone; pneumonia from an evaluation; and which physician, room,"],[21.4,"or treatment."],[22.34,"That's the core discipline of a logical agent:"],[26.14,"derive only what the facts and rules support."],[29.93,"One limitation shows up clearly, though."],[32.78,"Every sentence was about Maria specifically."],[35.62,"For another patient, we'd write every rule again."],[39.41,"In Chapter 8, first-order logic lets one rule cover every patient."]]},{"n":187,"title":"Putting It Together","marks":[],"caps":[[0.61,"Let's pull it all together."]]},{"n":188,"title":"Four mistakes almost every beginner makes","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,2.34],"t":6.87},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,1.43,6.04,2.34],"t":18.88},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.88,6.04,2.34],"t":28.29},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,3.88,6.04,2.34],"t":39.78}],"caps":[[0.7,"Four mistakes almost every beginner makes, so you can avoid them."],[6.45,"One:"],[6.97,"reading implies as \"causes\"."],[9.06,"It only checks truth values, and it's false only when the premise is true"],[16.37,"and the conclusion false."],[18.46,"Two:"],[18.98,"running a rule backward."],[21.07,"From A implies B and B, you can't conclude A."],[26.3,"Remember the sprinkler."],[27.87,"Three:"],[28.39,"treating \"not entailed\" as \"false\"."],[31.0,"Not entailed means unknown."],[33.09,"Square two, two was unknown:"],[35.7,"not proved safe, and not proved deadly."],[39.36,"Four:"],[39.88,"reading \"or\" as one-or-the-other."],[41.97,"Logical or includes both."]]},{"n":189,"title":"Chapter 7 in one slide","marks":[],"caps":[[0.69,"Here's every inference method from today on one slide."],[5.7,"Truth-table enumeration checks every world:"],[8.49,"sound and complete, but exponential."],[11.28,"Inference rules like Modus Ponens give short proofs."],[15.73,"Resolution cancels pairs and proves by contradiction:"],[19.63,"sound and complete."],[21.3,"Forward and backward chaining, for definite clauses:"],[25.2,"sound, complete, and linear."],[27.43,"DPLL:"],[27.99,"smart backtracking for SAT, complete."],[30.78,"And WalkSAT:"],[31.89,"fast local search, but incomplete."]]},{"n":190,"title":"Check your understanding","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.88,6.04,2.14],"t":8.92},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,1.88,6.04,2.14],"t":14.15},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.13,6.04,2.14],"t":17.0},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,4.13,6.04,2.14],"t":21.76}],"caps":[[0.46,"Four questions to check your understanding."],[3.31,"Pause the video, and answer all four before you go on."],[8.54,"One:"],[9.02,"after visiting two, one, why isn't two, two provably safe?"],[13.78,"Two:"],[14.25,"when is \"P implies Q\" false?"],[17.1,"Three:"],[17.58,"how does resolution prove that K B entails alpha?"],[21.86,"Four:"],[22.34,"forward or backward chaining for the question \"does Maria need monitoring?\""]]},{"n":191,"title":"Check your understanding · answers","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.68,1.43,6.04,2.34],"t":14.77},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,3.88,6.04,2.34],"t":24.38}],"caps":[[0.47,"The answers."],[1.35,"One:"],[1.78,"two of the three worlds that fit the knowledge base have a pit in"],[7.89,"two, two, so \"no pit in two, two\" is not entailed."],[12.69,"Two:"],[13.13,"\"P implies Q\" is false only when P is true and Q is false."],[19.24,"Three:"],[19.68,"resolution adds not alpha and derives the empty clause, a contradiction."],[24.48,"Four:"],[24.91,"backward chaining."],[25.79,"It's one specific question, so work back from the goal and only touch the"],[31.9,"relevant rules."]]},{"n":192,"title":"Practice and references","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,4.74],"t":0.94}],"caps":[[0.63,"For practice:"],[1.44,"write the wumpus knowledge base after visiting one, two,"],[5.05,"and prove by resolution that there's a pit in three, one."],[9.46,"Convert \"A if and only if, B or C\" to C N F,"],[14.68,"one step at a time."],[16.69,"Run forward and backward chaining on the Horn knowledge base, and record the order."],[22.3,"And run DPLL by hand on a sentence of your own."],[26.72,"For reading:"],[27.52,"Russell and Norvig, Chapter 7, and the original papers on DPLL, WalkSAT,"],[32.34,"and hard random SAT problems."]]},{"n":193,"title":"Questions?","marks":[],"caps":[[0.31,"That's Chapter 7."],[1.53,"A knowledge-based agent TELLs facts to its knowledge base and ASKs it what to"],[7.22,"do."],[7.62,"Entailment means a conclusion is true in every world where the knowledge is true."],[13.31,"We can check entailment by listing worlds, by proving it with inference rules and"],[18.99,"resolution, by chaining through rules, or by smart model checking with DPLL and WalkSAT."],[24.68,"In the wumpus world, those tools let an agent prove which squares are safe,"],[30.36,"and at Northgate, they let the hospital's AI conclude exactly what Maria's facts support."],[36.05,"Next time, Chapter 8:"],[37.67,"first-order logic, where one sentence can say \"every square next to a pit is"],[43.36,"breezy\", or \"every patient with difficulty breathing needs an urgent evaluation\"."],[47.83,"Thank you."],[48.64,"Bring your questions to class or to office hours."]]}]}]},{"id":"ch8","title":"Chapter 8 · First-Order Logic","sections":[{"id":"ch8-s1","title":"8.1 Why first-order logic?","dir":"ch8/s1","slides":[{"n":1,"title":"First-Order Logic","marks":[],"caps":[[22.79,"Welcome, everyone."],[23.94,"Today we start Chapter 8: First-Order Logic."],[28.57,"In Chapter 7, our agents reasoned with propositional logic, where every fact is a simple switch, true or false."],[36.71,"It worked, but it was clumsy."],[39.07,"Today we give our agents a much richer language, one that talks about things, the relationships between them, and about all of them at once."],[48.1,"As before, we go one small step at a time."],[51.71,"Every slide adds exactly one new idea."]]},{"n":2,"title":"Remember this from Chapter 7?","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.38,7.54,0.8],"t":8.54},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.16,7.54,0.8],"t":14.47},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,5.96,5.1,5.96],"t":21.23},{"k":"box","g":[8.33,1.38,4.44,4.64],"t":24.64}],"caps":[[0.73,"Remember this from Chapter 7?"],[2.64,"To tell the wumpus agent how breezes work, we had to write a separate sentence for every square."],[8.64,"Square one, one is breezy if and only if one of its neighbours has a pit."],[14.57,"Square two, one is breezy if and only if one of its neighbours has a pit."],[20.36,"And so on, sixteen times."],[22.75,"Propositional logic has no way to say \"any square\"."],[27.08,"So the same idea gets copied, once for each square."]]},{"n":3,"title":"One sentence instead of sixteen","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.96,12.6,2.96],"t":3.21},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.13,12.24,1.64],"t":5.21},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.93,12.24,1.24],"t":20.12}],"caps":[[0.65,"Here's the same knowledge in first-order logic, in one sentence."],[5.31,"Read it aloud like this: for every square s, s is breezy exactly when there is some square r that is next to s and has a pit."],[15.21,"One sentence, and it covers every square in the cave, of any size."],[20.22,"Don't worry about the symbols yet."],[22.18,"By the end of today, you will read this sentence easily."]]},{"n":4,"title":"Today’s roadmap","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.48,3.99,2.39],"t":3.01},{"k":"box","g":[4.63,1.48,3.99,2.39],"t":8.56},{"k":"box","g":[8.73,1.48,3.99,2.39],"t":20.32},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.03,3.99,2.39],"t":27.19},{"k":"box","g":[4.63,4.03,3.99,2.39],"t":34.32},{"k":"box","g":[8.73,4.03,3.99,2.39],"t":39.2}],"caps":[[0.75,"Here's our path for today."],[3.11,"Section eight point one: why we need more than propositional logic."],[8.66,"Section eight point two: the language itself."],[11.84,"We'll build a tiny world, learn to read sentences in it, and meet the two quantifiers, \"for all\" and \"there exists\"."],[20.42,"Section eight point three: using the language, for families, numbers, and the wumpus world."],[27.29,"Section eight point four: knowledge engineering, a seven-step process, applied to a digital circuit."],[34.42,"Then Northgate Hospital, where one rule will cover every patient."],[39.16,"And we finish by putting it all together."]]},{"n":5,"title":"By the end of this lecture, you should be able to…","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,1.59],"t":3.34},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,1.43,6.04,1.59],"t":9.71},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.13,6.04,1.59],"t":15.49},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,3.13,6.04,1.59],"t":21.03},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.83,6.04,1.59],"t":25.19},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,4.83,6.04,1.59],"t":29.79}],"caps":[[0.64,"Here's what you should be able to do by the end."],[3.44,"One: name the objects, relations, and functions in an everyday sentence."],[9.82,"Two: evaluate a first-order sentence in a small world, step by step."],[15.59,"Three: check \"for all\" and \"there exists\" one object at a time."],[21.13,"Four: avoid the two classic quantifier mistakes."],[25.29,"Five: explain why the order of nested quantifiers matters."],[29.7,"And six: follow the seven steps of knowledge engineering for a small system."]]},{"n":6,"title":"How this lecture works","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,3.94,3.74],"t":2.37},{"k":"box","g":[4.68,1.43,3.94,3.74],"t":5.7},{"k":"box","g":[8.83,1.43,3.94,3.74],"t":8.31},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,6.06,12.6,6.06],"t":13.55}],"caps":[[0.66,"Same rules as last time."],[2.47,"One step per slide, with what changed highlighted."],[5.8,"Words before symbols."],[7.44,"And when you see the pause badge, stop the video and make a guess before you continue."],[13.38,"Our running examples: a tiny world with two kings, the wumpus cave, a digital circuit, and Northgate Hospital."]]},{"n":7,"title":"Why First-Order Logic?","marks":[],"caps":[[0.58,"Section eight point one: why first-order logic?"]]},{"n":8,"title":"The hospital has the same problem","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.38,8.14,0.86],"t":20.96},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.23,8.14,0.86],"t":22.08},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.08,8.14,0.86],"t":23.37},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.93,8.14,0.86],"t":24.47},{"k":"box","g":[8.93,1.38,3.84,4.14],"t":28.3}],"caps":[[0.63,"Northgate Hospital has the same problem."],[3.32,"Say the rule is: a fever and low blood pressure make a patient urgent."],[8.11,"In propositional logic, Maria's name is baked into every symbol."],[12.58,"Fever underscore Maria, low B P underscore Maria, urgent underscore Maria."],[19.64,"So we need one copy for Maria, one for James, one for Aisha, one for Wei, and a new copy every time someone is admitted."],[28.4,"Until somebody writes that copy, the rule simply doesn't apply to the new patient."]]},{"n":9,"title":"The hospital has the same problem","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.96,12.6,2.96],"t":3.53},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,3.13,6.04,2.94],"t":10.5}],"caps":[[0.59,"In first-order logic, it's one sentence: for every patient p, if p has a fever and low blood pressure, then p is urgent."],[10.6,"What changed?"],[11.81,"Maria is no longer baked into the symbol name."],[14.78,"She's an object, and the rule talks about any object."],[18.2,"New patients are covered the moment they arrive."]]},{"n":10,"title":"Three ingredients of the world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[4.46,1.45,4.88,1.55],"t":11.48},{"k":"ul","g":[9.5,2.81,12.4,2.81],"t":23.35}],"caps":[[0.66,"First-order logic sees the world the way ordinary language does."],[4.8,"It has three ingredients."],[6.28,"Let's take them one at a time."],[9.0,"Ingredient one: objects."],[11.0,"These are the nouns: the things the world contains."],[15.32,"Maria."],[16.34,"Ward B."],[17.51,"Doctor Okafor."],[19.01,"The wumpus."],[20.32,"Square two, one."],[21.78,"Even the number three."],[23.45,"Each one can be given a name."]]},{"n":11,"title":"Three ingredients of the world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[4.46,3.2,4.88,1.55],"t":18.1},{"k":"ul","g":[9.5,4.56,12.4,4.56],"t":28.6}],"caps":[[0.64,"Ingredient two: relations."],[3.29,"These are like adjectives and verbs, and each one is simply true or false."],[8.12,"A relation on one object is called a property."],[11.85,"Patient of Maria: Maria is a patient."],[14.93,"A relation can also connect two objects."],[18.2,"Adjacent, square one one, square two one: those squares are next to each other."],[23.83,"Treats, Okafor, Maria: Doctor Okafor treats Maria."],[28.7,"Each of these is either true or false."]]},{"n":12,"title":"Three ingredients of the world","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[4.46,4.95,4.88,1.55],"t":7.62},{"k":"ul","g":[9.5,6.31,12.4,6.31],"t":17.07}],"caps":[[0.62,"Ingredient three: functions."],[3.31,"A function takes objects and points to exactly one object."],[7.72,"Bed of Maria points to bed twelve."],[10.94,"Left leg of John points to John's left leg."],[14.17,"Mother of Sofia points to Maria."],[17.17,"Here's the key difference."],[18.79,"A relation is true or false."],[21.46,"A function is neither."],[23.4,"It names a thing."],[24.03,"\"Bed of Maria\" isn't a statement; it's a way of saying \"that bed\"."]]},{"n":13,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[],"caps":[[0.93,"Pause and predict."],[2.44,"Here's an everyday sentence: Doctor Okafor treats every patient in Ward B."],[8.75,"Which word names a relation?"],[11.02,"A: Okafor."],[13.13,"B: treats."],[15.02,"C: Ward B."],[17.34,"Pause the video, and choose."]]},{"n":14,"title":"Answer: “treats” is a relation","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[0.46,1.5,2.13,1.4],"t":10.62},{"k":"ring","g":[2.51,1.5,2.13,1.4],"t":12.51},{"k":"ring","g":[4.56,1.5,2.13,1.4],"t":15.04},{"k":"ring","g":[6.61,1.5,2.13,1.4],"t":19.52},{"k":"ring","g":[8.66,1.5,2.13,1.4],"t":23.16},{"k":"ring","g":[10.71,1.5,2.13,1.4],"t":27.11},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,5.26,12.6,5.26],"t":28.8}],"caps":[[0.62,"The answer is B: treats."],[3.28,"It's true or false of two objects, Doctor Okafor and a patient."],[8.16,"Let's label the whole sentence."],[10.72,"Okafor is an object."],[12.61,"Treats is a relation."],[14.42,"\"Every\" is something new, a quantifier, which we'll meet in section eight point two."],[19.62,"Patient is a relation on one object, a property."],[23.16,"\"In\" is a relation between a patient and a ward."],[27.03,"And Ward B is an object."],[28.9,"Here's the whole sentence in first-order logic."],[31.95,"For every p, if p is a patient and p is in Ward B, then Okafor treats p."],[39.2,"By the end of section eight point two, you'll read every symbol."]]},{"n":15,"title":"What a logic assumes the world contains","marks":[{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,2.12,12.1,0.62],"t":9.02},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,2.74,12.1,0.62],"t":17.82},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,3.36,12.1,0.62],"t":28.54},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,3.98,12.1,0.62],"t":30.73},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,4.6,12.1,0.62],"t":36.79}],"caps":[[0.79,"Every logic makes two assumptions."],[3.16,"First, what exists in the world."],[5.64,"Second, what the agent can believe about each fact."],[9.12,"Propositional logic assumes the world is made only of facts, and each fact is believed true, false, or unknown."],[17.92,"First-order logic keeps those same three beliefs, but sees a richer world: facts, objects, and relations."],[26.18,"Other logics make other choices."],[28.64,"Temporal logic adds time."],[30.83,"Probability theory, which we'll meet later, lets belief be any degree from zero to one."],[36.89,"Fuzzy logic lets truth itself come in degrees."],[40.57,"Today, we stay with first-order logic."]]},{"n":16,"title":"Recap · 8.1","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,2.54],"t":10.38},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.13,6.04,2.54],"t":12.55},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,4.13,6.04,2.54],"t":16.34}],"caps":[[0.67,"Recap of section eight point one."],[2.75,"Propositional logic has to copy one rule for every square or every patient."],[8.98,"First-order logic sees objects, which are the nouns; relations, which are true or false about objects; and functions, which point to exactly one object."],[20.26,"Next, let's build a tiny world and learn to read sentences in it."]]}]},{"id":"ch8-s2","title":"8.2 Syntax and semantics","dir":"ch8/s2","slides":[{"n":17,"title":"Syntax and Semantics","marks":[],"caps":[[0.78,"Section eight point two: syntax and semantics."],[4.82,"This is the heart of the chapter."],[6.82,"We'll build a tiny world, then learn to read sentences about it, one piece at a time."]]},{"n":18,"title":"A tiny world, built one piece at a time","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[1.16,3.05,1.43,1.35],"t":13.68},{"k":"ring","g":[4.76,3.05,1.43,1.35],"t":15.84}],"caps":[[0.61,"Let's build a tiny world, the one the textbook uses."],[4.33,"It's about two brothers from English history, Richard the Lionheart and King John."],[9.04,"We'll keep it small so we can check everything by hand."],[13.04,"It starts with two objects: Richard, and John."]]},{"n":19,"title":"A tiny world, built one piece at a time","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[4.76,1.35,1.43,1.35],"t":0.93}],"caps":[[0.85,"Add a third object: a crown."],[3.57,"Objects don't have to be people."],[5.74,"Anything we want to talk about can be an object."]]},{"n":20,"title":"A tiny world, built one piece at a time","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[1.16,4.9,1.43,1.35],"t":1.51},{"k":"ring","g":[4.76,4.9,1.43,1.35],"t":3.39},{"k":"ul","g":[7.7,4.81,12.6,4.81],"t":14.75}],"caps":[[0.66,"Add two more: Richard's left leg, and John's left leg."],[4.92,"Strange objects, but the textbook includes them to show off functions, as you'll see."],[10.43,"That's five objects in total."],[12.64,"The set of all objects in a world is called its domain."],[16.38,"This world's domain has five objects."]]},{"n":21,"title":"A tiny world, built one piece at a time","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[2.38,2.98,2.59,1.49],"t":2.16},{"k":"ul","g":[7.7,4.81,12.6,4.81],"t":11.3}],"caps":[[0.74,"Now the relations."],[2.26,"First, brother."],[3.94,"Richard is John's brother, and John is Richard's brother."],[7.89,"Two arrows, one each way."],[10.7,"Formally, a relation is just a set of pairs: the pairs for which it's true."],[16.02,"Brother is true for Richard and John, and for John and Richard."],[20.34,"False for every other pair."]]},{"n":22,"title":"A tiny world, built one piece at a time","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[5.03,2.48,1.94,0.74],"t":2.0}],"caps":[[0.71,"Second relation: on head."],[3.22,"The crown is on John's head."],[5.42,"That's the only pair for which on head is true."]]},{"n":23,"title":"A tiny world, built one piece at a time","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[0.21,3.4,1.18,0.62],"t":4.05},{"k":"ring","g":[5.99,3.15,1.18,0.62],"t":5.31},{"k":"ring","g":[5.99,3.65,1.18,0.62],"t":6.67},{"k":"ring","g":[5.99,1.7,1.13,0.62],"t":9.43}],"caps":[[0.64,"Now the properties, relations on a single object."],[4.15,"Person is true of Richard and John."],[6.77,"King is true only of John."],[9.21,"And Crown is true of the crown."],[11.81,"Notice: King is false of Richard, and Person is false of the crown and the legs."]]},{"n":24,"title":"A tiny world, built one piece at a time","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[1.53,4.23,4.74,0.84],"t":8.86},{"k":"box","g":[7.53,4.93,5.24,1.34],"t":10.92}],"caps":[[0.78,"Finally, a function: left leg."],[3.85,"It points each person to exactly one object, their own left leg."],[8.78,"The dashed arrows show it."],[11.02,"That's the complete world."],[12.57,"Five objects, two relations between objects, three properties, and one function."],[18.46,"Everything we say next is checked against this picture."]]},{"n":25,"title":"Names are not the things themselves","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,4.64],"t":6.8},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,4.64],"t":24.94},{"k":"ul","g":[6.9,4.76,12.6,4.76],"t":31.31}],"caps":[[0.65,"Here's a subtle but important point."],[2.87,"The names we write are not the things themselves."],[6.9,"On the left are symbols, which is syntax."],[10.06,"Constant symbols name objects: Richard, John."],[13.85,"Predicate symbols name relations: brother, on head, person, king, crown."],[21.01,"Function symbols name functions: left leg."],[25.04,"On the right are the actual things in the picture, which is semantics."],[29.24,"The matching between names and things is called an interpretation: which name points to which thing."]]},{"n":26,"title":"An interpretation: which name points to which thing","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[7.53,1.38,5.24,0.86],"t":2.93},{"k":"box","g":[7.53,2.2,5.24,0.86],"t":6.33},{"k":"box","g":[7.53,3.02,5.24,0.86],"t":9.62},{"k":"box","g":[7.53,3.84,5.24,0.86],"t":13.13},{"k":"box","g":[7.53,4.68,5.24,1.59],"t":16.22},{"k":"ring","g":[4.76,1.35,1.43,1.35],"t":22.77}],"caps":[[0.69,"Here's the intended interpretation."],[3.03,"The name Richard points to the object Richard."],[6.43,"The name John points to the object John."],[9.72,"King points to the set containing John."],[12.8,"And left leg points to the leg function."],[16.15,"But here's the surprise: logic alone doesn't force this matching."],[20.6,"An interpretation where the name Richard points to the crown is perfectly allowed."],[25.62,"That's why a single sentence can be true in one interpretation and false in another, just like in Chapter 7."]]},{"n":27,"title":"Why there are so many possible models","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,4.64],"t":2.8},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,4.64],"t":13.32}],"caps":[[0.67,"This has a big consequence."],[2.9,"In Chapter 7, a model was just a true or false value for each symbol, so n symbols gave two to the n models."],[10.96,"Large, but you could list them."],[13.42,"In first-order logic, a model is a domain of objects plus an interpretation of every name."],[19.74,"The domain can have any number of objects, and the names can point anywhere."],[24.14,"The number of models is unbounded."],[26.62,"We can't check entailment by listing models anymore."],[29.75,"That's why Chapter 9 is all about inference rules."]]},{"n":28,"title":"Terms: ways to point at an object","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,2.34],"t":7.42},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,2.34],"t":12.7},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.93,12.24,2.14],"t":19.67}],"caps":[[0.55,"Before we write sentences, we need terms."],[3.7,"A term is any way of pointing at an object."],[7.52,"A constant is a term: the name John points to the object John."],[12.67,"And a function applied to terms is also a term: left leg of John points to John's left leg."],[19.77,"That's useful: we can talk about John's left leg without giving it a name of its own."],[24.73,"Bed of Maria, mother of Sofia, work the same way."]]},{"n":29,"title":"Evaluating the term  LeftLeg(John)","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[7.53,2.38,5.24,1.04],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[4.76,3.05,1.43,1.35],"t":5.32},{"k":"ring","g":[4.76,4.9,1.43,1.35],"t":13.35},{"k":"box","g":[7.53,3.53,5.24,2.74],"t":18.49}],"caps":[[0.31,"Let's evaluate the term left leg of John, step by step."],[5.42,"Start inside: the name John points to the object John."],[10.53,"Then follow the left leg arrow from John."],[13.45,"It lands on John's left leg."],[15.63,"That's what the term refers to."],[17.86,"It works just like arithmetic: evaluate the innermost part first, then apply the function."]]},{"n":30,"title":"Atomic sentences: a predicate applied to terms","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[1.16,3.05,1.43,1.35],"t":12.61},{"k":"ring","g":[4.76,3.05,1.43,1.35],"t":12.61},{"k":"box","g":[2.38,2.98,2.59,1.49],"t":17.46},{"k":"box","g":[7.53,4.98,5.24,1.14],"t":20.22}],"caps":[[0.79,"Now sentences."],[2.42,"The simplest is an atomic sentence: a predicate applied to terms."],[6.93,"It's true or false."],[9.07,"Brother, Richard, John."],[11.41,"To check it, look up the two objects, then ask: is this pair in the brother relation?"],[17.56,"There's a brother arrow from Richard to John."],[20.32,"So it's true."]]},{"n":31,"title":"Atomic sentences: a predicate applied to terms","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[1.16,3.05,1.43,1.35],"t":3.16},{"k":"ring","g":[5.99,3.65,1.18,0.62],"t":7.68},{"k":"box","g":[7.53,4.98,5.24,1.14],"t":9.36}],"caps":[[0.84,"Next: King of Richard."],[3.26,"Look up Richard, and ask: is Richard in the king set?"],[7.05,"No."],[7.78,"Only John is a king."],[9.46,"So this sentence is false."]]},{"n":32,"title":"Atomic sentences: a predicate applied to terms","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[4.76,4.9,1.43,1.35],"t":8.85},{"k":"box","g":[7.53,4.98,5.24,1.14],"t":14.71}],"caps":[[0.66,"A trickier one: Person of left leg of John."],[4.82,"First evaluate the term inside: left leg of John points to John's left leg."],[11.18,"Then ask: is that leg a person?"],[14.2,"No."],[14.81,"So it's false."],[16.33,"Notice: terms point at objects, and the predicate around them gives the true or false answer."]]},{"n":33,"title":"Complex sentences: the connectives from Chapter 7","marks":[{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,2.18,8.0,0.68],"t":7.69},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,2.86,8.0,0.68],"t":13.06},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,3.54,8.0,0.68],"t":18.28},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,4.22,8.0,0.68],"t":23.59},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,4.9,8.0,0.68],"t":32.46}],"caps":[[0.73,"Complex sentences use the same five connectives as Chapter 7, with exactly the same truth tables."],[7.79,"Not King of Richard: King of Richard is false, so this is true."],[13.16,"King of John and Brother Richard John: both true, so true."],[18.38,"King of Richard or King of John: John is a king, so true."],[23.69,"King of Richard implies Person of Richard: the premise is false, so the implication is true, remember the promise."],[31.83,"And King of John implies Crown of John: the premise is true, but John is not a crown, so this one is false."]]},{"n":34,"title":"“For all”: the universal quantifier ∀","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.86,12.6,2.86],"t":9.72},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.03,6.04,3.04],"t":12.29},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,3.03,6.04,3.04],"t":22.54}],"caps":[[0.62,"Now the new power: quantifiers."],[3.58,"The first is \"for all\", written as an upside-down A."],[8.53,"For all x, King of x implies Person of x."],[12.39,"Read it aloud: for every object x, if x is a king, then x is a person."],[19.03,"In short: all kings are persons."],[22.64,"The letter x is a variable, a placeholder, like a blank in a form."],[27.58,"\"For all x\" means: try every object in the domain in that blank."],[32.77,"The sentence is true only if it holds for every single one."],[36.79,"Let's check, one object at a time."]]},{"n":35,"title":"Checking  ∀x King(x) ⇒ Person(x)  object by object","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.98,8.14,0.69],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[6.06,1.95,2.68,0.75],"t":7.26}],"caps":[[0.64,"Object one: Richard."],[2.92,"Is Richard a king?"],[4.81,"No."],[5.24,"So the if-part is false, and the implication is true."],[9.27,"Remember: a promise isn't broken if the if-part doesn't happen."],[13.71,"Richard passes."]]},{"n":36,"title":"Checking  ∀x King(x) ⇒ Person(x)  object by object","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.53,8.14,0.69],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[6.06,2.5,2.68,0.75],"t":7.66}],"caps":[[0.62,"Object two: John."],[2.87,"Is John a king?"],[4.42,"Yes."],[5.47,"Is John a person?"],[6.83,"Yes."],[7.76,"True and true: the implication holds."],[10.86,"John passes."]]},{"n":37,"title":"Checking  ∀x King(x) ⇒ Person(x)  object by object","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.08,8.14,0.69],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[6.06,3.05,2.68,0.75],"t":5.82}],"caps":[[0.74,"Object three: the crown."],[3.23,"Is the crown a king?"],[5.32,"No."],[5.75,"So the implication is true."],[7.71,"The crown passes."]]},{"n":38,"title":"Checking  ∀x King(x) ⇒ Person(x)  object by object","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.63,8.14,0.69],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[6.06,3.6,2.68,0.75],"t":5.11}],"caps":[[0.71,"Object four: Richard's left leg."],[3.86,"Not a king, so the implication is true."],[6.97,"It passes."]]},{"n":39,"title":"Checking  ∀x King(x) ⇒ Person(x)  object by object","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.18,8.14,0.69],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[6.06,4.15,2.68,0.75],"t":3.53}],"caps":[[0.61,"Object five: John's left leg."],[3.63,"Not a king either."],[5.08,"It passes."]]},{"n":40,"title":"Verdict: true for every object, so the ∀ sentence is TRUE","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[8.76,1.4,4.08,2.2],"t":0.72},{"k":"box","g":[8.83,3.68,3.94,2.49],"t":9.5}],"caps":[[0.82,"All five objects pass, so the sentence \"for all x, King of x implies Person of x\" is true in this world."],[9.6,"The rule: \"for all\" is true when every object passes."],[14.23,"A single failure, a single counterexample, makes it false."],[18.48,"Just like entailment in Chapter 7."]]},{"n":41,"title":"∀ is a big AND","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,3.76,12.6,3.76],"t":3.91},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.93,12.24,2.14],"t":8.5}],"caps":[[0.84,"Here's a helpful way to think about it."],[2.88,"\"For all\" is a long \"and\", with one copy of the sentence for each object."],[8.6,"That's exactly what we wrote by hand in Chapter 7: one copy per square."],[13.78,"Now the logic writes the copies for us."]]},{"n":42,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[],"caps":[[0.68,"Pause and predict."],[2.55,"A student wants to say \"all kings are persons\", but writes: for all x, King of x and Person of x."],[10.8,"With \"and\" instead of \"implies\"."],[13.68,"Is this sentence true in our tiny world?"],[16.9,"Check the first object yourself before you continue."]]},{"n":43,"title":"Mistake 1: ∀ with ∧","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.98,8.14,0.69],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[8.76,1.4,4.08,2.2],"t":13.62}],"caps":[[0.75,"The answer is B: false."],[3.34,"Check the first object, Richard."],[5.29,"Is Richard a king and a person?"],[7.74,"He's a person, but not a king."],[10.16,"So the \"and\" is false, and the whole \"for all\" fails immediately."],[15.27,"We can stop."]]},{"n":44,"title":"What the ∧ version really says","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,3.14],"t":0.73},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,4.16,6.4,4.16],"t":10.51},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,3.14],"t":13.63},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,6.06,12.6,6.06],"t":23.53}],"caps":[[0.83,"What does the \"and\" version really say?"],[3.63,"For every object, it's a king and it's a person."],[7.5,"Everything is a king, and everything is a person."],[10.61,"Even the crown would have to be a king!"],[13.73,"The correct version, with implies, says: anything that is a king is a person."],[19.83,"It makes no claim at all about non-kings."],[22.55,"So here's the rule of thumb: \"for all\" goes with \"implies\"."]]},{"n":45,"title":"“There exists”: the existential quantifier ∃","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.86,12.6,2.86],"t":6.58},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.03,6.04,3.04],"t":11.89},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,3.03,6.04,3.04],"t":20.67}],"caps":[[0.9,"The second quantifier is \"there exists\", written as a backwards E."],[6.68,"There exists x such that Crown of x and On Head x John."],[11.99,"Read it aloud: there is some object x that is a crown and is on John's head."],[17.77,"In short: John wears a crown."],[20.77,"To check it, try objects one at a time."],[23.87,"As soon as one object works, we've found a witness, and the sentence is true."],[28.83,"We can stop."]]},{"n":46,"title":"Checking  ∃x Crown(x) ∧ OnHead(x, John)","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.98,8.14,0.69],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.65,"Object one: Richard."],[2.98,"Is Richard a crown?"],[4.41,"No."],[4.85,"So this object doesn't work."],[7.55,"But for \"there exists\", one failure means nothing."],[11.28,"Keep looking."]]},{"n":47,"title":"Checking  ∃x Crown(x) ∧ OnHead(x, John)","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.53,8.14,0.69],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.47,"Object two: John."],[2.79,"Is John a crown?"],[4.68,"No."],[5.28,"Keep looking."]]},{"n":48,"title":"Checking  ∃x Crown(x) ∧ OnHead(x, John)","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.08,8.14,0.69],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[8.76,1.4,4.08,2.2],"t":10.22}],"caps":[[0.84,"Object three: the crown."],[3.46,"Is it a crown?"],[4.75,"Yes."],[5.29,"Is it on John's head?"],[7.31,"Yes."],[8.14,"Both true!"],[9.33,"The crown is our witness."],[11.21,"The sentence is true, and we don't even need to check the legs."]]},{"n":49,"title":"∃ is a big OR","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,3.76,12.6,3.76],"t":5.18},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.93,6.04,2.14],"t":13.21},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,3.93,6.04,2.14],"t":19.29}],"caps":[[0.62,"Just as \"for all\" is a big \"and\", \"there exists\" is a big \"or\": one copy per object, joined by \"or\"."],[10.32,"At least one copy must be true."],[13.31,"So: for all needs every object to pass, and one failure makes it false."],[19.39,"There exists needs just one object to pass, and one witness makes it true."]]},{"n":50,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[],"caps":[[0.63,"Pause and predict."],[2.55,"Does Richard wear a crown?"],[4.5,"Look at the picture: he doesn't."],[6.94,"But a student writes: there exists x such that Crown of x implies On Head x Richard."],[14.19,"With \"implies\" instead of \"and\"."],[17.0,"What does this sentence evaluate to?"],[19.76,"Try the first object, Richard."]]},{"n":51,"title":"Mistake 2: ∃ with ⇒","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.98,8.14,0.69],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[8.76,1.4,4.08,2.2],"t":12.47}],"caps":[[0.66,"The answer is A: true!"],[3.07,"And that's the problem."],[4.3,"Take the first object, Richard."],[6.61,"Is Richard a crown?"],[8.27,"No."],[8.87,"So the if-part is false, and the implication is true."],[12.57,"Richard becomes a witness, and the whole sentence comes out true."],[17.38,"The sentence claims Richard wears a crown, and it's \"true\", even though Richard wears no crown at all."]]},{"n":52,"title":"The fix: ∃ goes with ∧","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,3.14],"t":0.74},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,3.14],"t":9.93},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,6.06,12.6,6.06],"t":21.22}],"caps":[[0.84,"The implication version is true as soon as any object is not a crown."],[5.97,"So it's almost always true, and useless."],[10.03,"The \"and\" version needs one object that is a crown and is on Richard's head."],[15.32,"No such object exists, so it's false."],[18.62,"That's the correct answer."],[20.54,"So now we have both halves of the rule: \"for all\" goes with \"implies\"."],[26.03,"\"There exists\" goes with \"and\"."],[28.89,"Write it down; it will save you many mistakes."]]},{"n":53,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[],"caps":[[1.01,"Pause and predict."],[2.81,"Which sentence says: some patient in Ward B has a fever?"],[7.63,"A: there exists p, patient and in Ward B and fever."],[13.48,"B: there exists p, patient and in Ward B implies fever."],[19.73,"C: for all p, patient and in Ward B and fever."]]},{"n":54,"title":"Answer: A","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,3.99,4.64],"t":0.52},{"k":"box","g":[4.65,1.43,3.99,4.64],"t":9.12},{"k":"box","g":[8.77,1.43,3.99,4.64],"t":20.12}],"caps":[[0.62,"The answer is A."],[2.42,"There exists goes with \"and\": one patient who is in Ward B and has a fever."],[9.22,"B uses \"implies\" with \"there exists\", so it's true as soon as anything at all isn't a patient in Ward B, like a chair or a bed."],[18.49,"Useless."],[20.22,"C uses \"for all\" with \"and\", so it says everything in the hospital is a feverish patient in Ward B."],[27.69,"Including the chairs."]]},{"n":55,"title":"Two quantifiers in one sentence","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.66,1.45,1.48,1.4],"t":13.45},{"k":"ring","g":[4.86,4.5,1.48,1.4],"t":15.12},{"k":"ring","g":[0.46,4.5,1.48,1.4],"t":16.68},{"k":"box","g":[7.23,3.73,5.54,2.44],"t":18.34}],"caps":[[0.69,"Real knowledge often needs two quantifiers at once."],[4.18,"To see how they work, here's a second tiny world: three people, Ana, Ben, and Cy."],[10.58,"Each loves exactly one other person."],[13.55,"Ana loves Ben."],[15.23,"Ben loves Cy."],[16.78,"Cy loves Ana."],[18.44,"Here are two sentences that look almost identical."],[22.39,"For all x, there exists y, Loves x y."],[26.73,"And: there exists y, for all x, Loves x y."],[31.32,"Same symbols, different order."],[33.69,"Let's see whether they mean the same thing."]]},{"n":56,"title":"Read them aloud","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,4.64],"t":3.56},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,4.64],"t":15.11}],"caps":[[0.65,"Read them aloud, left to right."],[3.66,"The first: for every person x, there is someone y whom x loves."],[9.24,"In other words: everyone loves somebody."],[12.13,"And each person can pick a different somebody."],[15.21,"The second: there is one person y such that every x loves y."],[20.92,"In other words: there is somebody whom everyone loves."],[24.71,"One single y, shared by everybody."],[28.26,"Those are very different claims."],[30.61,"Let's check each one in our world."]]},{"n":57,"title":"Check  ∀x ∃y Loves(x, y):  one x at a time","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[7.23,1.98,5.54,0.74],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[4.86,4.5,1.48,1.4],"t":18.73}],"caps":[[0.86,"Check the first sentence."],[2.56,"\"For all x\" means: try every person as x."],[6.79,"\"There exists y\" means: for that x, find one person they love."],[20.09,"x equals Ana."],[21.51,"Does Ana love somebody?"],[23.66,"Yes, Ben."],[23.66,"Ana passes."]]},{"n":58,"title":"Check  ∀x ∃y Loves(x, y):  one x at a time","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[7.23,2.58,5.54,0.74],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[0.46,4.5,1.48,1.4],"t":4.33}],"caps":[[0.77,"x equals Ben."],[2.12,"Does Ben love somebody?"],[4.43,"Yes, Cy."],[5.29,"Notice Ben's witness is different from Ana's."],[8.5,"That's allowed."],[9.48,"Ben passes."]]},{"n":59,"title":"Check  ∀x ∃y Loves(x, y):  one x at a time","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[7.23,3.18,5.54,0.74],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[2.66,1.45,1.48,1.4],"t":4.46}],"caps":[[0.64,"x equals Cy."],[2.27,"Does Cy love somebody?"],[4.56,"Yes, Ana."],[5.94,"Cy passes."]]},{"n":60,"title":"Verdict: everyone loves somebody — TRUE","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[7.16,4.1,5.68,2.1],"t":8.02}],"caps":[[0.86,"All three people pass, so \"for all x, there exists y, Loves x y\" is true."],[8.12,"Everyone loves somebody."]]},{"n":61,"title":"Check  ∃y ∀x Loves(x, y):  one candidate y at a time","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[7.23,1.98,5.54,0.74],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[2.66,1.45,1.48,1.4],"t":12.6}],"caps":[[0.74,"Now the second sentence."],[2.45,"\"There exists y\" comes first, so we try each person as the one shared y."],[8.08,"Then \"for all x\" asks: does everybody love that y?"],[12.7,"Candidate y equals Ana."],[15.5,"Does everyone love Ana?"],[17.3,"Only Cy does."],[18.66,"Ana herself doesn't, and Ben doesn't."],[21.53,"So Ana fails as the shared y."]]},{"n":62,"title":"Check  ∃y ∀x Loves(x, y):  one candidate y at a time","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[4.86,4.5,1.48,1.4],"t":0.58},{"k":"box","g":[7.23,2.58,5.54,0.74],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.68,"Candidate y equals Ben."],[3.05,"Does everyone love Ben?"],[4.73,"Only Ana does."],[6.09,"Ben fails."]]},{"n":63,"title":"Check  ∃y ∀x Loves(x, y):  one candidate y at a time","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[7.23,3.18,5.54,0.74],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[0.46,4.5,1.48,1.4],"t":0.87}],"caps":[[0.97,"Candidate y equals Cy."],[3.48,"Does everyone love Cy?"],[5.16,"Only Ben does."],[6.64,"Cy fails."]]},{"n":64,"title":"Verdict: nobody is loved by everyone — FALSE","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[6.66,1.4,6.18,2.1],"t":0.62},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.68,12.24,2.44],"t":10.36},{"k":"ring","g":[0.46,1.4,6.18,2.1],"t":17.19}],"caps":[[0.72,"No candidate works, so \"there exists y, for all x, Loves x y\" is false."],[7.96,"Nobody is loved by everyone."],[10.46,"Same symbols, opposite answers."],[13.28,"The order of \"for all\" and \"there exists\" matters."],[17.29,"When \"for all\" comes first, each x picks its own y."],[21.48,"When \"there exists\" comes first, one y must work for every x."],[26.79,"One small note: two quantifiers of the same kind, like \"for all x, for all y\", can be swapped freely."],[34.48,"It's only mixing the two kinds that makes order matter."]]},{"n":65,"title":"∀ and ∃ are two sides of one coin","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.76,12.6,2.76],"t":12.08},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.93,12.24,3.04],"t":22.0}],"caps":[[0.61,"\"For all\" and \"there exists\" are two sides of one coin."],[6.01,"\"Everybody dislikes parsnips\" means the same as \"there is nobody who likes parsnips\"."],[12.18,"In symbols: for all x, not Likes x Parsnips, is equivalent to: not, there exists x, Likes x Parsnips."],[22.1,"Push a \"not\" through a quantifier, and the quantifier flips: \"for all\" becomes \"there exists\", and \"there exists\" becomes \"for all\"."]]},{"n":66,"title":"The four duality rules","marks":[{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,2.25,12.1,0.75],"t":2.43},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,3.0,12.1,0.75],"t":3.88},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,3.75,12.1,0.75],"t":4.15},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,4.5,12.1,0.75],"t":10.53},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.33,12.24,0.99],"t":25.7}],"caps":[[0.37,"Here are all four rules."],[2.53,"Nobody likes it."],[3.98,"Not everyone likes it, which means someone doesn't."],[7.36,"Everyone likes it, which means nobody doesn't."],[10.63,"And someone likes it, which means not everyone doesn't."],[15.04,"If they look familiar, they should: these are De Morgan's rules from Chapter 7."],[20.41,"\"For all\" is a big \"and\", \"there exists\" is a big \"or\", so De Morgan stretches to cover every object."]]},{"n":67,"title":"Equality: two names, one object","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.76,12.6,2.76],"t":3.92},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":6.49},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":14.48}],"caps":[[1.01,"One more piece of the language: equality."],[4.02,"Father of John equals Henry."],[6.59,"The equals sign says the two terms point to the same object."],[10.6,"\"The father of John\" and \"Henry\" are one person."],[14.58,"We need it because different names can point to the same thing."],[18.37,"Equality lets us say so, or say that two things are different."]]},{"n":68,"title":"“Richard has at least two brothers”","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,12.24,1.14],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.77,"Let's use it."],[1.95,"Suppose we want to say: Richard has at least two brothers."],[6.17,"A first try: Brother John Richard, and Brother Geoffrey Richard."]]},{"n":69,"title":"“Richard has at least two brothers”","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.53,12.24,1.14],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.52,"But wait."],[1.54,"Remember that logic alone doesn't stop two names from pointing to the same object."],[6.63,"In some interpretation, John and Geoffrey could be the same person."],[11.05,"Then Richard would have only one brother, and our sentence would still be true."]]},{"n":70,"title":"“Richard has at least two brothers”","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.63,12.24,1.14],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.62,"The fix: there exist x and y, x is Richard's brother, y is Richard's brother, and x is not equal to y."],[9.9,"The \"not equal\" is what guarantees two different brothers."]]},{"n":71,"title":"A shortcut many systems use: database semantics","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,3.99,4.64],"t":7.94},{"k":"box","g":[4.65,1.43,3.99,4.64],"t":16.27},{"k":"box","g":[8.77,1.43,3.99,4.64],"t":25.2}],"caps":[[0.64,"Many practical systems, like databases, take a shortcut called database semantics."],[6.52,"Three assumptions."],[8.04,"Unique names: different names always mean different objects, so John and Geoffrey are automatically two people."],[16.37,"Closed world: anything not known to be true is false."],[20.77,"If the knowledge base doesn't say Pete is Richard's brother, he isn't."],[25.3,"Domain closure: the only objects are the ones that are named."],[29.95,"Under these assumptions, \"Brother John Richard and Brother Geoffrey Richard\" really does mean exactly two brothers."],[37.42,"Standard first-order logic does not assume these, so you must say what you mean."]]},{"n":72,"title":"Recap · 8.2","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,3.973,2.54],"t":3.39},{"k":"box","g":[8.797,1.43,3.973,2.54],"t":4.01},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.13,3.973,2.54],"t":4.15},{"k":"box","g":[8.797,4.13,3.973,2.54],"t":29.17}],"caps":[[0.81,"Recap of section eight point two."],[3.42,"A model is a domain of objects plus an interpretation of every name."],[8.21,"Terms point to objects, and atomic sentences are true or false."],[13.2,"\"For all\" is a big \"and\": every object must pass, and it goes with \"implies\"."],[20.05,"\"There exists\" is a big \"or\": one witness is enough, and it goes with \"and\"."],[26.18,"When the two are mixed, their order matters."],[29.11,"And equality means \"same object\"."],[32.63,"Next, let's use the language to build real knowledge bases."]]}]},{"id":"ch8-s3","title":"8.3 Using first-order logic","dir":"ch8/s3","slides":[{"n":73,"title":"Using First-Order Logic","marks":[],"caps":[[0.59,"Section eight point three: using first-order logic."],[4.39,"Now that we can read the language, let's build knowledge bases with it."]]},{"n":74,"title":"TELL and ASK, now with objects","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,12.24,1.09],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.71,"The knowledge base works just as in Chapter 7: we TELL it sentences, and ASK it questions."],[7.34,"First, we TELL it a fact: John is a king."]]},{"n":75,"title":"TELL and ASK, now with objects","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.48,12.24,1.09],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.75,"Then we TELL it a rule: for all x, if x is a king, then x is a person."]]},{"n":76,"title":"TELL and ASK, now with objects","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.53,12.24,1.09],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.83,12.24,1.34],"t":4.91}],"caps":[[0.68,"Now we ASK: is John a person?"],[3.53,"The answer is yes."],[5.01,"Nobody told the knowledge base that directly, but it's entailed: it's true in every model where the knowledge base is true."],[12.98,"Chapter 9 shows exactly how a machine finds that answer."]]},{"n":77,"title":"ASKVARS: “who?” instead of “yes or no?”","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.76,12.6,2.76],"t":8.3},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":13.04},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":27.89}],"caps":[[0.82,"Often we don't want a yes or no."],[3.36,"We want to know who."],[5.14,"That's ASK VARS."],[7.21,"ASK VARS, Person of x, asks: for which x is this true?"],[13.15,"The answer is a substitution, written x slash John."],[17.7,"Read it as: x can be John."],[20.2,"If the knowledge base also knew Richard was a person, we'd get x slash Richard as a second answer."],[27.99,"That's what a hospital agent really needs: not \"is someone urgent?\""],[32.49,", but \"which patients are urgent?\""]]},{"n":78,"title":"A family knowledge base","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.76,1.3,3.58,1.3],"t":6.72},{"k":"ring","g":[0.56,3.1,3.58,1.3],"t":9.84},{"k":"box","g":[7.03,1.38,5.74,2.74],"t":14.32},{"k":"box","g":[7.03,4.23,5.74,1.94],"t":21.67}],"caps":[[0.6,"Let's build a family knowledge base, the textbook's kinship domain."],[5.16,"Here's a small family: Grace and Henry are the parents of Elena and Tom."],[9.94,"Elena and Luis are the parents of Sofia and Leo."],[14.42,"We store only three kinds of base fact: parent, female, and male."],[19.65,"One parent fact per arrow."],[21.77,"Every other family word, mother, sibling, grandparent, we will define from those."]]},{"n":79,"title":"Defining family words, one at a time","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[7.2,3.71,12.6,3.71],"t":3.15},{"k":"ring","g":[2.76,3.1,1.38,1.3],"t":11.28},{"k":"ring","g":[0.86,4.9,1.38,1.3],"t":17.52}],"caps":[[0.64,"Definition one: mother."],[3.25,"For all m and c, m is the mother of c if and only if m is female and m is a parent of c."],[11.38,"Check it on the tree: Elena is female, and Elena is a parent of Sofia."],[17.62,"So Elena is Sofia's mother."]]},{"n":80,"title":"Defining family words, one at a time","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[7.2,3.71,12.6,3.71],"t":3.26},{"k":"box","g":[7.03,1.98,5.74,1.74],"t":12.78},{"k":"ring","g":[0.86,4.9,1.38,1.3],"t":22.05},{"k":"ring","g":[3.06,4.9,1.38,1.3],"t":22.05}],"caps":[[0.66,"Definition two: sibling."],[3.36,"x and y are siblings if and only if x is not equal to y, and there exists some p who is a parent of both."],[12.88,"Notice the \"not equal\"."],[15.04,"Without it, Sofia would be her own sibling, because she shares a parent with herself."],[20.14,"Equality earns its keep."],[22.15,"Check: Sofia and Leo are different, and share the parent Elena."],[26.8,"So they're siblings."]]},{"n":81,"title":"Defining family words, one at a time","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[7.2,3.71,12.6,3.71],"t":3.54},{"k":"ring","g":[2.76,1.3,1.38,1.3],"t":15.27},{"k":"ring","g":[0.86,4.9,1.38,1.3],"t":18.49}],"caps":[[0.6,"Definition three: grandparent."],[3.64,"g is a grandparent of c if and only if there exists some p, where g is a parent of p, and p is a parent of c."],[13.03,"Two parent arrows in a row."],[15.37,"Grace, to Elena, to Sofia."],[18.35,"So Grace is Sofia's grandparent."],[21.09,"Notice \"there exists\" goes with \"and\", our rule of thumb."]]},{"n":82,"title":"Asking a question about the family","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[7.03,1.38,5.74,1.19],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[2.76,1.3,1.38,1.3],"t":4.0},{"k":"ring","g":[0.86,4.9,1.38,1.3],"t":4.78}],"caps":[[0.66,"Let's ASK a question and answer it step by step: is Grace a grandparent of Sofia?"]]},{"n":83,"title":"Asking a question about the family","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[7.03,2.53,5.74,1.19],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.72,"Unfold the definition of grandparent."],[3.5,"We need some person p, where Grace is a parent of p, and p is a parent of Sofia."],[9.74,"\"There exists p\": so we look for a witness."]]},{"n":84,"title":"Asking a question about the family","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[7.03,3.68,5.74,1.19],"t":0.6},{"k":"ring","g":[4.96,3.1,1.38,1.3],"t":0.64}],"caps":[[0.74,"Try p equals Tom."],[2.92,"Is Grace a parent of Tom?"],[4.92,"Yes."],[5.9,"Is Tom a parent of Sofia?"],[8.01,"No."],[8.64,"Tom is not a witness."],[10.57,"For \"there exists\", that's fine; keep looking."]]},{"n":85,"title":"Asking a question about the family","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[2.76,3.1,1.38,1.3],"t":0.5},{"k":"box","g":[7.03,4.83,5.74,1.19],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.6,"Try p equals Elena."],[2.85,"Grace is a parent of Elena, yes."],[5.49,"Elena is a parent of Sofia, yes."],[8.15,"Witness found!"],[9.8,"Grace is Sofia's grandparent."]]},{"n":86,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[],"caps":[[0.72,"Pause and predict."],[2.57,"We ask: ASK VARS, Sibling, Sofia, x."],[7.09,"Who are Sofia's siblings?"],[9.9,"Which substitutions come back?"]]},{"n":87,"title":"Answer: only { x / Leo }","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[7.03,1.38,5.74,1.64],"t":0.58},{"k":"box","g":[7.03,3.08,5.74,1.54],"t":8.24},{"k":"box","g":[7.03,4.68,5.74,1.54],"t":13.24}],"caps":[[0.68,"The answer is A: only x slash Leo."],[4.62,"Leo is different from Sofia and shares her parents."],[8.34,"Sofia herself is ruled out by the \"not equal\" in the definition."],[13.35,"And Tom is Sofia's uncle, not her sibling: they don't share a parent."],[17.89,"I checked this by running the definitions over the whole family in code."]]},{"n":88,"title":"Axioms, definitions and theorems","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,3.99,4.64],"t":2.2},{"k":"box","g":[4.65,1.43,3.99,4.64],"t":6.68},{"k":"box","g":[8.77,1.43,3.99,4.64],"t":15.96}],"caps":[[0.62,"Some vocabulary."],[2.3,"Axioms are the basic facts and rules we TELL the knowledge base."],[6.78,"Definitions are axioms of a special shape, \"word of x if and only if something\", that say exactly what a word means."],[15.82,"And theorems are sentences that follow from the axioms, like \"Grace is Sofia's grandparent\"."],[22.11,"We never store theorems; we derive them when we need them."]]},{"n":89,"title":"Numbers from almost nothing: Peano axioms","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,12.24,0.99],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.89,"First-order logic can even build the natural numbers from almost nothing."],[5.36,"These are the Peano axioms."],[8.0,"Axiom one: zero is a natural number."]]},{"n":90,"title":"Numbers from almost nothing: Peano axioms","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.38,12.24,0.99],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.43,12.24,1.54],"t":7.29}],"caps":[[0.75,"Axiom two: for every n, if n is a natural number, so is S of n."],[7.39,"S means \"successor\", the next number."],[10.36,"So one is S of zero, two is S of S of zero, and so on."]]},{"n":91,"title":"Numbers from almost nothing: Peano axioms","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.33,12.24,0.99],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.67,"Now addition."],[2.09,"For every m, zero plus m equals m."],[6.09,"Adding zero changes nothing."]]},{"n":92,"title":"Numbers from almost nothing: Peano axioms","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.28,12.24,0.99],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.73,"And for every m and n, S of m, plus n, equals S of, m plus n."],[7.49,"In words: to add one-more-than-m to n, add m to n, then take one more."],[14.16,"That's all of addition."]]},{"n":93,"title":"Computing 1 + 1 with the axioms","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,12.24,0.99],"t":6.7},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.38,12.24,0.99],"t":10.6},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.33,12.24,0.99],"t":17.09},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.28,12.24,0.99],"t":25.47}],"caps":[[0.77,"Let's compute one plus one using only the axioms."],[4.77,"One is S of zero, so we start with: S of zero, plus S of zero."],[10.7,"Axiom four moves one S outside: S of, zero plus S of zero."],[17.19,"Axiom three says zero plus anything is that thing: so we get S of S of zero."],[23.64,"And S of S of zero is our name for two."],[27.23,"One plus one is two, derived from scratch."],[31.14,"Slow, but every step is guaranteed."]]},{"n":94,"title":"Sets and lists, briefly","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,4.64],"t":5.15},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,4.64],"t":15.87}],"caps":[[0.74,"Sets and lists work the same way: a few axioms define them."],[5.25,"For sets: the empty set, membership, and a rule that two sets are equal exactly when each is a subset of the other, meaning they have the same members."],[15.97,"Lists are like sets, but order matters and repeats are allowed."],[20.67,"The wumpus agent uses a list of five items for its percept every turn."],[25.17,"Let's go back to the cave."]]},{"n":95,"title":"The wumpus world, take two: the percept","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.76,12.6,2.76],"t":4.38},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":10.82},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,2.93,6.04,3.14],"t":16.28}],"caps":[[0.67,"Back to the wumpus world, now in first-order logic."],[4.48,"Each turn the agent TELLs one percept sentence: a list of five percepts and the time step."],[10.92,"At time five, it smelled a stench, felt a breeze, and saw glitter."],[16.38,"Time steps are objects too."],[18.6,"So one rule covers every time step: for all t, and any values in the other slots, if the second slot of the percept at time t is Breeze, then there's a breeze at time t."]]},{"n":96,"title":"Squares are objects: [x, y]","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,3.06,12.6,3.06],"t":9.44},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.23,6.04,2.84],"t":17.08},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,3.23,6.04,2.84],"t":23.09}],"caps":[[1.01,"Squares are objects too, written as a pair: column x, row y."],[6.45,"And \"next to\" becomes one definition."],[9.54,"For all x, y, a, b: square x y is adjacent to square a b if and only if they're in the same column and one row apart, or in the same row and one column apart."],[23.2,"In Chapter 7 we listed every neighbour by hand."],[26.76,"This one definition works for a four-by-four cave, or a hundred-by-hundred cave."]]},{"n":97,"title":"One rule about breezes","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.86,12.6,2.86],"t":5.25},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.03,6.04,3.04],"t":13.26},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,3.03,6.04,3.04],"t":17.29}],"caps":[[0.66,"And here's the sentence from the very start of the lecture."],[3.71,"Now you can read it."],[5.35,"For every square s, s is breezy exactly when there is some square r that is adjacent to s and has a pit."],[13.36,"Notice: \"there exists\" goes with \"and\"."],[17.39,"It replaces all sixteen breeze sentences from Chapter 7, in one line."]]},{"n":98,"title":"Two directions for a rule","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,3.54],"t":3.23},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,3.54],"t":11.65},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.13,12.24,1.09],"t":22.85}],"caps":[[0.81,"A rule can point in two directions."],[3.33,"Diagnostic rules go from effect to cause: a breeze tells me a pit is nearby, like a doctor reasoning from symptoms."],[11.75,"Causal rules go from cause to effect: a pit makes every neighbouring square breezy."],[17.92,"That's how the world works."],[20.38,"The \"if and only if\" rule we wrote says both at once."]]},{"n":99,"title":"The agent deduces safe squares","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,12.24,1.09],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.75,"Let's watch the agent reason, step by step, just like Chapter 7, but with first-order sentences."],[7.86,"The agent is in square one, one, and feels no breeze."],[12.1,"So: not Breezy, square one one."]]},{"n":100,"title":"The agent deduces safe squares","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.48,12.24,1.09],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.75,"The breeze rule says: breezy if and only if some neighbour has a pit."],[6.49,"Since one, one is not breezy, it is not true that some neighbour has a pit."],[12.1,"Not, there exists r, adjacent to one one and a pit."]]},{"n":101,"title":"The agent deduces safe squares","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.53,12.24,1.09],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.7,"Now duality from section eight point two."],[3.91,"Push the \"not\" inside, and \"there exists\" flips to \"for all\"."],[9.32,"For every r, if r is adjacent to one, one, then r has no pit."]]},{"n":102,"title":"The agent deduces safe squares","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.58,12.24,1.09],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.72,"Finally, which squares are adjacent to one, one?"],[4.7,"The adjacency definition gives exactly two: one, two and two, one."],[10.13,"So neither has a pit."],[11.91,"Same conclusion as Chapter 7, from far fewer sentences."]]},{"n":103,"title":"Recap · 8.3","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.13,3.973,2.54],"t":17.61},{"k":"box","g":[4.663,4.13,3.973,2.54],"t":19.75}],"caps":[[0.7,"Recap of section eight point three."],[3.32,"TELL and ASK work as before, and ASK VARS returns substitutions."],[8.78,"We defined family words from three base predicates, and \"not equal\" stopped Sofia being her own sibling."],[16.1,"A handful of axioms build numbers and sets."],[19.29,"And in the wumpus world, one breeze rule and one adjacency definition replace dozens of sentences."],[26.35,"Next: how to build a knowledge base for something new, step by step."]]}]},{"id":"ch8-s4","title":"8.4 Knowledge engineering","dir":"ch8/s4","slides":[{"n":104,"title":"Knowledge Engineering","marks":[],"caps":[[0.68,"Section eight point four: knowledge engineering."],[4.2,"How do you build a knowledge base for a brand-new problem?"],[7.53,"There's a seven-step process, and we'll follow it on a digital circuit."]]},{"n":105,"title":"Seven steps of knowledge engineering","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.48,2.99,2.24],"t":7.85},{"k":"box","g":[3.63,1.48,2.99,2.24],"t":12.09},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.48,2.99,2.24],"t":14.78},{"k":"box","g":[9.83,1.48,2.99,2.24],"t":19.52},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.88,2.99,2.24],"t":23.09},{"k":"box","g":[3.63,3.88,2.99,2.24],"t":26.1},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,3.88,2.99,2.24],"t":29.14}],"caps":[[0.69,"A knowledge engineer builds a knowledge base the way a programmer builds software, and there are seven steps."],[7.95,"One: identify the questions the knowledge base must answer."],[12.19,"Two: assemble the relevant knowledge."],[14.88,"Three: choose a vocabulary of predicates, functions, and constants."],[19.62,"Four: encode the general knowledge about the domain."],[23.19,"Five: encode the specific problem."],[26.2,"Six: pose queries and get answers."],[29.24,"Seven: debug."],[31.45,"Let's walk through all seven on one example: a one-bit full adder, a small digital circuit."]]},{"n":106,"title":"Step 1 · Identify the questions","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[0.51,1.6,0.73,2.85],"t":7.0},{"k":"ring","g":[1.96,1.5,1.33,1.05],"t":15.29},{"k":"ring","g":[4.26,2.1,1.33,1.05],"t":15.29},{"k":"ring","g":[1.96,4.9,1.33,1.05],"t":17.03},{"k":"ring","g":[4.26,3.65,1.33,1.05],"t":17.03},{"k":"ring","g":[5.81,4.25,1.33,1.05],"t":18.48},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,3.88,4.54,2.34],"t":19.98}],"caps":[[0.75,"Step one: identify the questions."],[3.66,"Here's the circuit, a one-bit full adder."],[7.1,"It takes three bits, two inputs plus a carry coming in, and outputs a sum bit and a carry bit."],[13.88,"It has five gates: two X O R gates, two AND gates, and one OR gate."],[20.08,"Our questions: does it add correctly?"],[22.9,"What are the outputs for given inputs?"],[25.65,"And which inputs give a particular output?"]]},{"n":107,"title":"Step 2 · Assemble the knowledge","marks":[{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,2.25,8.2,0.75],"t":8.62},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,3.0,8.2,0.75],"t":14.5},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,3.75,8.2,0.75],"t":16.55},{"k":"box","g":[9.03,1.43,3.74,3.14],"t":22.61}],"caps":[[0.7,"Step two: assemble the knowledge."],[3.68,"What does a circuit expert know that matters?"],[6.65,"How each gate type behaves."],[8.73,"An AND gate outputs one when both inputs are one."],[14.6,"An OR gate outputs one when at least one input is one."],[16.65,"An X O R gate outputs one when the inputs are different."],[21.18,"And just as important: what we leave out."],[24.19,"Wire colours, gate sizes, cost, speed."],[27.9,"None of them matter for our questions."],[30.16,"Knowing what to ignore is half the job."]]},{"n":108,"title":"Step 3 · Choose a vocabulary","marks":[{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,2.3,12.1,0.8],"t":3.38},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,3.1,12.1,0.8],"t":10.49},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,3.9,12.1,0.8],"t":17.66},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,4.7,12.1,0.8],"t":20.63},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,5.58,12.24,0.79],"t":24.7}],"caps":[[0.61,"Step three: choose a vocabulary."],[3.48,"Gate of X one says X one is a gate, and Type of X one equals X O R gives its type."],[10.59,"In and Out are functions that name a gate's terminals: In one, X one is the first input of X one."],[17.76,"Connected says a wire joins two terminals."],[20.73,"And Signal of t gives the signal at terminal t."],[24.8,"Notice that Type and Signal are functions, not relations: each gate has exactly one type, and each terminal carries exactly one signal."]]},{"n":109,"title":"Step 4 · Encode general knowledge","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,12.24,1.14],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.93,"Step four: encode the general knowledge, the rules true of every circuit."],[6.67,"One axiom per slide."],[8.94,"Axiom one: for all terminals t one and t two, if they're connected, they carry the same signal."],[16.34,"A wire carries the same signal at both ends."]]},{"n":110,"title":"Step 4 · Encode general knowledge","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.53,12.24,1.14],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.75,"Axiom two: every signal is either one or zero."],[5.32,"And one is not equal to zero."],[8.16,"That second part looks silly, but remember equality: logic doesn't know two names mean different things unless we say so."],[16.59,"We'll see why it matters in step seven."]]},{"n":111,"title":"Step 4 · Encode general knowledge","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.63,12.24,1.14],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.88,"Axiom three, for AND gates: the output is zero exactly when some input is zero."],[8.2,"Notice \"there exists\" goes with an equality here, inside the \"if and only if\"."]]},{"n":112,"title":"Step 4 · Encode general knowledge","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.73,12.24,1.14],"t":0.6}],"caps":[[0.74,"Axiom four, for X O R gates: the output is one exactly when the two inputs are different."],[7.83,"There's a similar axiom for OR gates."],[10.38,"These few axioms describe every full adder, and every other circuit built from these gates."]]},{"n":113,"title":"Step 5 · Encode this specific circuit","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[8.23,1.38,4.54,2.14],"t":4.16},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,3.58,4.54,2.64],"t":13.24},{"k":"arrow","g":[3.15,1.775,4.4,2.2],"t":17.04}],"caps":[[0.73,"Step five: encode this specific circuit."],[4.26,"Every gate gets its type: X one and X two are X O R, A one and A two are AND, O one is OR."],[13.16,"And every wire becomes one Connected sentence."],[16.29,"For example, output one of X one is connected to input one of X two."],[21.48,"C one names the whole circuit, so input one of C one is the circuit's first input."],[28.03,"General knowledge from step four, plus this specific description, is the complete knowledge base."]]},{"n":114,"title":"Step 6 · Pose a query: inputs 1, 1, 0","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[0.51,1.6,0.73,2.85],"t":3.14},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,1.38,4.54,1.64],"t":6.84},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,3.13,4.54,3.04],"t":16.85}],"caps":[[0.72,"Step six: pose queries."],[3.24,"Set the inputs to one, one, and zero, and ask: what are the sum and the carry?"],[9.79,"We know the answer we hope for."],[11.83,"One plus one plus zero is two, which in binary is one zero."],[16.79,"So we expect sum zero, carry one."],[19.58,"Let's watch the inference work through the gates, one at a time."]]},{"n":115,"title":"Step 6 · Signals flow gate by gate","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[1.96,1.5,1.33,1.05],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,2.08,4.54,1.74],"t":3.19},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,3.88,4.54,2.34],"t":14.88}],"caps":[[0.61,"Gate X one is an X O R gate."],[3.29,"Its inputs are wired to circuit inputs one and two, which are both one."],[8.16,"By axiom one, the wires carry those signals."],[11.73,"By the X O R axiom, the inputs are the same, so X one outputs zero."]]},{"n":116,"title":"Step 6 · Signals flow gate by gate","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[1.96,4.9,1.33,1.05],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,2.08,4.54,1.74],"t":3.01},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,3.88,4.54,2.34],"t":7.05}],"caps":[[0.71,"Gate A one is an AND gate, also fed by inputs one and two."],[5.82,"Both are one, so A one outputs one."]]},{"n":117,"title":"Step 6 · Signals flow gate by gate","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[4.26,2.1,1.33,1.05],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,2.08,4.54,1.74],"t":3.2},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,3.88,4.54,2.34],"t":10.48},{"k":"ul","g":[6.9,2.885,7.5,2.885],"t":15.51}],"caps":[[0.81,"Gate X two is an X O R gate fed by X one's output, zero, and circuit input three, also zero."],[9.04,"The inputs are the same, so X two outputs zero."],[12.62,"X two's output is wired to the sum."],[15.61,"So the sum is zero."]]},{"n":118,"title":"Step 6 · Signals flow gate by gate","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[4.26,3.65,1.33,1.05],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,2.08,4.54,1.74],"t":2.8},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,3.88,4.54,2.34],"t":7.55}],"caps":[[0.67,"Gate A two is an AND gate fed by X one, zero, and input three, zero."],[6.75,"Not both one, so A two outputs zero."]]},{"n":119,"title":"Step 6 · Signals flow gate by gate","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[5.81,4.25,1.33,1.05],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,2.08,4.54,1.74],"t":1.66},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,3.88,4.54,2.34],"t":8.39},{"k":"ul","g":[7.4,5.035,8.1,5.035],"t":12.43}],"caps":[[0.72,"Gate O one is an OR gate fed by A two, zero, and A one, one."],[6.65,"At least one input is one, so O one outputs one."],[10.88,"It's wired to the carry."],[12.53,"So the carry is one."]]},{"n":120,"title":"Answer: Sum = 0, Carry = 1 — the circuit adds correctly","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[8.16,1.35,4.68,1.7],"t":0.57},{"k":"box","g":[8.23,3.13,4.54,3.04],"t":8.61}],"caps":[[0.67,"Sum zero, carry one."],[2.99,"That's binary one zero, which is two."],[5.96,"The circuit adds correctly for these inputs."],[8.71,"Every step used only the general axioms and the wiring, so the same reasoning works for any inputs."]]},{"n":121,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[],"caps":[[0.57,"Pause and predict."],[2.42,"Now the question runs backwards."],[4.7,"Which inputs give sum zero and carry one?"],[8.37,"Think about binary: sum zero, carry one means the total is two."]]},{"n":122,"title":"Answer: any two inputs are 1","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.83,3.94,1.74],"t":7.75},{"k":"box","g":[4.68,2.83,3.94,1.74],"t":8.29},{"k":"box","g":[8.83,2.83,3.94,1.74],"t":11.44},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.73,12.24,1.44],"t":15.21}],"caps":[[0.58,"The answer is B: any two inputs equal to one."],[4.59,"ASK VARS returns three substitutions: one, one, zero."],[9.84,"One, zero, one."],[11.54,"And zero, one, one."],[13.67,"Each adds up to two."],[15.32,"Here's the power of a knowledge base."],[18.15,"We never wrote new axioms to run the circuit backwards."],[21.63,"The same knowledge answers questions in both directions."],[25.18,"A computer program that simulates the circuit can only run it forwards."]]},{"n":123,"title":"Checking every input: the full truth table","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[9.03,1.38,3.74,4.79],"t":5.71},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,3.65,8.2,0.55],"t":14.41},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,4.75,8.2,0.55],"t":14.41},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,5.3,8.2,0.55],"t":14.41}],"caps":[[0.69,"To be sure the circuit adds correctly, ask about all eight input combinations."],[5.81,"In every row, carry times two plus sum equals the total of the inputs."],[11.26,"I checked every row by running the axioms in code."],[14.51,"The three teal rows are the answers to the backwards query."]]},{"n":124,"title":"Step 7 · Debug","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,3.14],"t":3.18},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,3.14],"t":22.36},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,6.16,12.6,6.16],"t":33.56}],"caps":[[0.62,"Step seven: debug."],[2.49,"Here's a classic bug: forgetting the axiom \"one is not equal to zero\"."],[8.33,"Ask for X one's output with inputs one and zero."],[12.16,"The X O R axiom needs to know the inputs are different, and without \"one is not equal to zero\", the knowledge base can't prove that."],[20.11,"So no answer comes back."],[22.46,"How do you find a bug like that?"],[24.65,"Ask small questions, one gate at a time."],[28.13,"Where an answer that should be there is missing, look for a missing or wrong axiom."],[33.66,"Bugs in a knowledge base are not crashes."],[36.82,"The knowledge base just can't conclude what it should."]]},{"n":125,"title":"Recap · 8.4","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,2.54],"t":4.79},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,2.54],"t":8.43}],"caps":[[0.95,"Recap of section eight point four."],[3.59,"Knowledge engineering has seven steps: questions, knowledge, vocabulary, general axioms, the specific problem, queries, and debugging."],[15.55,"We kept the general axioms separate from this circuit's wiring."],[19.34,"And one knowledge base answered questions in both directions."],[23.05,"Now let's bring first-order logic to Northgate Hospital."]]}]},{"id":"ch8-s5","title":"Northgate Hospital and wrap-up","dir":"ch8/s5","slides":[{"n":126,"title":"Case Study: Northgate Hospital","marks":[],"caps":[[0.66,"Back to Northgate Hospital."],[2.69,"In Chapter 7, the hospital's AI had to write every rule separately for Maria Santos."],[8.77,"Now Maria is an object, and every rule covers every patient."]]},{"n":127,"title":"From Chapter 7 to Chapter 8","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,3.34],"t":0.71},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,3.34],"t":13.58},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,6.16,12.6,6.16],"t":22.58}],"caps":[[0.81,"In Chapter 7, the triage rule had Maria's name baked into it: difficulty breathing underscore Maria implies urgent evaluation underscore Maria."],[11.65,"One copy per patient."],[13.68,"In Chapter 8: for all p, difficulty breathing of p implies urgent evaluation of p."],[20.57,"One rule, every patient."],[22.68,"Maria stops being part of a symbol name and becomes an object the rules can talk about."]]},{"n":128,"title":"Scenario 1 · Which patients must the ER see?","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.38,12.24,0.86],"t":4.52},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.2,12.24,0.86],"t":11.8},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.02,12.24,0.86],"t":18.58},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.84,12.24,0.86],"t":23.39},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,6.01,12.6,6.01],"t":26.55}],"caps":[[0.66,"Scenario one."],[2.16,"The knowledge base has two rules."],[4.62,"Rule one: for every patient p, if p has difficulty breathing, p needs an urgent evaluation."],[11.9,"Rule two: for every p, if p needs an urgent evaluation, an emergency physician sees p."],[18.68,"And some facts: Maria, James, and Wei are patients."],[23.49,"Maria and James have difficulty breathing."],[26.65,"We ask: ASK VARS, seen by E R of x."],[31.12,"Which patients must the emergency physician see?"],[33.97,"Predict the answer before we work through it."]]},{"n":129,"title":"Scenario 1 · Try each patient in the rules","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.13,8.34,0.84],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[9.03,1.43,3.74,4.74],"t":15.5}],"caps":[[0.86,"The rules start with \"for all p\", so we try each patient in the blank."],[6.21,"p equals Maria."],[8.38,"Maria is a patient with difficulty breathing, so rule one fires: urgent evaluation of Maria."],[15.24,"Then rule two fires: the emergency physician must see Maria."]]},{"n":130,"title":"Scenario 1 · Try each patient in the rules","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.83,8.34,0.84],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[9.03,1.43,3.74,4.74],"t":9.07}],"caps":[[0.59,"p equals James."],[2.14,"Same facts, same rules: urgent evaluation of James, so the E R must see James."],[9.17,"Notice we didn't write a single new rule for James."]]},{"n":131,"title":"Scenario 1 · Try each patient in the rules","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.53,8.34,0.84],"t":0.6},{"k":"box","g":[9.03,1.43,3.74,4.74],"t":6.89}],"caps":[[0.61,"p equals Wei."],[2.29,"Wei is a patient, but nothing says Wei has difficulty breathing."],[6.99,"Rule one can't fire, so nothing follows about Wei."]]},{"n":132,"title":"Scenario 1 · The answer, and a new arrival","marks":[{"k":"ring","g":[0.46,1.4,12.38,1.3],"t":1.31},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.83,6.04,3.34],"t":8.47},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,2.83,6.04,3.34],"t":22.55}],"caps":[[0.74,"ASK VARS returns two substitutions: x slash Maria, and x slash James."],[7.14,"Not Wei."],[8.57,"Now a new patient, Aisha, arrives with difficulty breathing."],[13.23,"We TELL the knowledge base two facts: Aisha is a patient, and she has difficulty breathing."],[19.42,"Ask again, and Aisha appears too."],[22.65,"Two new facts, and zero new rules."],[26.1,"In Chapter 7, the hospital would have needed new copies of both rules for Aisha."]]},{"n":133,"title":"Scenario 2 · “Not entailed” means unknown","marks":[{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,2.61,12.6,2.61],"t":2.1},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.78,6.04,3.39],"t":10.61},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,2.78,6.04,3.39],"t":19.5}],"caps":[[0.64,"Scenario two."],[2.2,"The rule: for every patient p, if p has difficulty breathing and low oxygen, p needs continuous monitoring."],[10.71,"The knowledge base knows Maria has difficulty breathing, but nothing yet about her oxygen."],[16.81,"Ask: does Maria need monitoring?"],[19.6,"It's not entailed."],[20.01,"And here's the trap from Chapter 7: that does not mean Maria needs no monitoring."],[26.89,"It means the knowledge base doesn't know yet."],[29.59,"The rule needs both conditions, and only one is known."]]},{"n":134,"title":"Scenario 2 · A nurse records the oxygen level","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,12.24,1.09],"t":2.41},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,2.48,12.24,1.09],"t":5.9},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.53,12.24,1.09],"t":11.03},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,6.06,12.6,6.06],"t":13.99}],"caps":[[0.59,"A nurse records low oxygen."],[2.51,"TELL the knowledge base: low oxygen of Maria."],[6.0,"Now substitute p equals Maria into the rule."],[9.59,"Both conditions are known, so monitor of Maria is entailed."],[14.09,"Adding a fact turned \"unknown\" into \"proved\", and the rule itself needed no change."]]},{"n":135,"title":"Scenario 3 · Staffing: two sentences that look alike","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[6.33,1.38,6.44,2.29],"t":11.44},{"k":"box","g":[6.33,3.78,6.44,2.39],"t":20.25}],"caps":[[0.82,"Scenario three: staffing, and the order of quantifiers."],[5.39,"Three nurses, Mehta, Lee, and Diaz, each assigned to one ward."],[11.54,"The hospital's policy: for every ward w, there exists a nurse assigned to w."],[17.6,"Every ward has some nurse."],[20.35,"Now swap the order: there exists a nurse n, such that for every ward w, n is assigned to w."],[27.84,"That says one single nurse covers every ward."],[32.07,"Which one is true here?"]]},{"n":136,"title":"Scenario 3 · Check the policy ward by ward","marks":[{"k":"hl","g":[6.4,2.05,6.3,0.6],"t":3.25},{"k":"ring","g":[4.46,1.5,1.38,1.3],"t":3.25},{"k":"hl","g":[6.4,2.65,6.3,0.6],"t":6.22},{"k":"ring","g":[4.46,3.05,1.38,1.3],"t":6.22},{"k":"hl","g":[6.4,3.25,6.3,0.6],"t":7.92},{"k":"ring","g":[4.46,4.6,1.38,1.3],"t":7.92},{"k":"ring","g":[6.26,4.1,6.58,1.6],"t":11.91}],"caps":[[0.69,"Check the policy, ward by ward."],[3.35,"Ward A: Mehta is assigned."],[6.32,"Ward B: Lee."],[8.02,"Ward C: Diaz."],[9.85,"Each ward has its own witness."],[12.01,"So the policy is true."]]},{"n":137,"title":"Pause and predict","marks":[],"caps":[[0.65,"Pause and predict."],[2.57,"Is the swapped sentence true in this hospital?"],[5.59,"Is there one nurse assigned to every ward?"]]},{"n":138,"title":"Answer: False — no single nurse covers every ward","marks":[{"k":"hl","g":[6.4,2.05,6.3,0.6],"t":7.25},{"k":"ring","g":[0.76,1.5,1.38,1.3],"t":7.25},{"k":"hl","g":[6.4,2.65,6.3,0.6],"t":11.92},{"k":"ring","g":[0.76,3.05,1.38,1.3],"t":11.92},{"k":"hl","g":[6.4,3.25,6.3,0.6],"t":12.42},{"k":"ring","g":[0.76,4.6,1.38,1.3],"t":12.42},{"k":"box","g":[6.33,4.13,6.44,2.04],"t":15.83}],"caps":[[0.74,"The answer is false."],[2.52,"Try each nurse as the one shared n."],[7.35,"Mehta covers only Ward A."],[12.02,"Lee only Ward B."],[12.52,"Diaz only Ward C."],[13.05,"Nobody covers every ward."],[15.93,"Why does it matter?"],[17.33,"If someone wrote the policy with the order swapped, a staffing checker would demand one nurse in three wards at once, and report every schedule as a violation."],[26.81,"Same symbols, different order, very different hospital."]]},{"n":139,"title":"Northgate: derived — and cannot be derived","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,4.44],"t":5.09},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,4.44],"t":20.49},{"k":"ul","g":[0.7,6.76,12.6,6.76],"t":36.73}],"caps":[[0.7,"Let's collect what Northgate's agent can and can't conclude."],[5.19,"Derived: the emergency physician must see Maria and James, from one pair of rules."],[11.31,"Aisha is covered the moment she arrives."],[14.36,"Maria needs monitoring, once her oxygen is recorded."],[18.08,"And every ward has a nurse."],[20.59,"Cannot be derived: anything about Wei's breathing."],[24.43,"Monitoring for Maria before the oxygen reading: that's unknown, not false."],[29.96,"And the claim that one nurse covers all wards."],[32.76,"We worked these out by trying objects in the rules by hand."],[36.83,"Chapter 9 shows how a machine does it automatically, with unification and chaining."]]},{"n":140,"title":"Putting It Together","marks":[],"caps":[[0.61,"Let's pull it all together."]]},{"n":141,"title":"Four mistakes almost every beginner makes","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,2.34],"t":4.73},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,1.43,6.04,2.34],"t":13.81},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.88,6.04,2.34],"t":23.79},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,3.88,6.04,2.34],"t":31.89}],"caps":[[0.77,"Four mistakes almost every beginner makes."],[4.83,"One: \"for all\" with \"and\"."],[7.7,"It claims everything has both properties."],[10.57,"Use \"implies\" with \"for all\"."],[13.91,"Two: \"there exists\" with \"implies\"."],[17.35,"It's true for any object that fails the if-part."],[20.66,"Use \"and\" with \"there exists\"."],[23.89,"Three: swapping \"for all\" and \"there exists\"."],[27.7,"They say different things, so read left to right."],[31.99,"Four: forgetting \"not equal\"."],[36.01,"Two names may point to one object, so say \"different\" when you mean it."]]},{"n":142,"title":"Chapter 8 in one slide","marks":[{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,2.05,12.1,0.6],"t":3.73},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,2.65,12.1,0.6],"t":7.04},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,3.25,12.1,0.6],"t":11.85},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,3.85,12.1,0.6],"t":17.87},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,4.45,12.1,0.6],"t":23.42},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,5.05,12.1,0.6],"t":26.03},{"k":"hl","g":[0.6,5.65,12.1,0.6],"t":28.64}],"caps":[[0.69,"Here's Chapter 8 on one slide."],[3.83,"The world has objects, relations, and functions."],[7.14,"A model is a domain of objects plus an interpretation of every name."],[11.89,"\"For all\" means every object passes, a big \"and\", paired with \"implies\"."],[17.93,"\"There exists\" means one witness is enough, a big \"or\", paired with \"and\"."],[23.52,"When the two are mixed, order matters."],[26.13,"Equality means \"same object\"."],[28.54,"And knowledge engineering takes seven steps, from questions to debugging."]]},{"n":143,"title":"Check your understanding","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.88,6.04,2.14],"t":6.65},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,1.88,6.04,2.14],"t":12.69},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,4.13,6.04,2.14],"t":17.85},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,4.13,6.04,2.14],"t":22.56}],"caps":[[0.64,"Four questions to check your understanding."],[3.21,"Pause the video and answer all four."],[6.75,"One: in the tiny world, is \"for all x, Person of x\" true?"],[12.79,"Two: translate \"some nurse in Ward B is certified\"."],[17.95,"Three: why does the sibling definition need \"not equal\"?"],[22.66,"Four: the knowledge base says \"monitor Wei\" is not entailed."],[27.43,"Is Wei safe without monitoring?"]]},{"n":144,"title":"Check your understanding · answers","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,2.34],"t":2.68},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,1.43,6.04,2.34],"t":8.8},{"k":"box","g":[0.53,3.88,6.04,2.34],"t":20.28},{"k":"box","g":[6.68,3.88,6.04,2.34],"t":28.67}],"caps":[[0.78,"The answers."],[2.78,"One: no."],[4.4,"The crown is not a person, so it's a counterexample."],[8.9,"Two: there exists n, such that n is a nurse, and n is in Ward B, and n is certified."],[17.42,"\"There exists\" with \"and\"."],[20.38,"Three: without \"not equal\", everyone would be their own sibling, since everyone shares a parent with themselves."],[28.77,"Four: not necessarily."],[31.28,"\"Not entailed\" means unknown, not false."],[34.74,"The knowledge base just doesn't know yet."]]},{"n":145,"title":"Practice and references","marks":[{"k":"box","g":[0.53,1.43,6.04,4.74],"t":0.54},{"k":"box","g":[6.73,1.43,6.04,4.74],"t":21.91}],"caps":[[0.64,"For practice: translate ten English sentences into first-order logic, checking each for the two quantifier mistakes."],[8.45,"Define aunt, uncle, and cousin from parent, female, and male."],[12.91,"Write the wumpus stench rule in one sentence."],[16.37,"And give the full adder a broken gate, then find inputs that reveal it."],[22.01,"For reading: Russell and Norvig, Chapter 8; Genesereth and Nilsson, who introduced the circuits domain; and Enderton for the mathematics of logic."]]},{"n":146,"title":"Questions?","marks":[],"caps":[[0.98,"That's Chapter 8."],[3.08,"First-order logic sees a world of objects, relations, and functions."],[7.67,"\"For all\" checks every object; \"there exists\" needs just one witness."],[13.02,"Their order matters, and equality lets us say when two things are the same, or different."],[18.48,"With this language, one sentence replaced sixteen in the wumpus cave, one knowledge base answered a circuit's questions in both directions, and one rule covered every patient at Northgate."],[29.98,"Next time, Chapter 9: inference in first-order logic."],[34.79,"How a machine actually finds these answers, with unification, chaining, and resolution."],[41.29,"Thank you."],[42.19,"Bring your questions to class or to office hours."]]}]}]}]};
